window.PMU={"courses":[{"id":"bootcamp","title":"The 7-Day Product Evaluation Bootcamp","short":"Bootcamp","intro":"<p>A one-week intensive to give you the vocabulary, frameworks and evaluation craft for Product Manager / Product Owner evaluation work, like micro1&#x27;s PM/PO contractor role. You&#x27;ve been doing much of this for years, from UAT at Netmarks to your own client work since 2017, without the formal names. This course gives it names, structure and the precision interviewers and reviewers look for.</p>","chapters":[{"id":"bc-how-to-use-this-course","title":"How to use this course","html":"<p>Study about 3 hours a day for 7 days: 1 hour learning, 1 hour practising on your own past work, 1 hour writing. Each day ends with exercises; do them in writing, because this role is judged entirely on written output.</p><p><strong>An honest word.</strong> One week won&#x27;t replace a master&#x27;s degree. What it can do is close the gap that matters here: you already have the experience, and what you&#x27;re missing is the vocabulary, the frameworks and the habit of being precise on paper. That&#x27;s learnable in a week.</p><p><strong>Daily rhythm</strong></p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Block</th><th>Time</th><th>What you do</th></tr><tr><td>Learn</td><td>60 min</td><td>Read the day&#x27;s section. Underline every term you couldn&#x27;t have defined yesterday</td></tr><tr><td>Translate</td><td>60 min</td><td>Pick one real project you did (CRM build, chatbot funnel, DMS rollout) and rewrite it using the day&#x27;s terms</td></tr><tr><td>Practise</td><td>60 min</td><td>Do the day&#x27;s exercises in writing. Then say one answer out loud in under 2 minutes</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>The one habit to build all week.</strong> Every judgment you make, written or spoken, follows this shape:</p><ol><li><strong>Verdict</strong>: what you decided.</li><li><strong>Rule</strong>: the criterion or requirement you applied.</li><li><strong>Evidence</strong>: the exact words, line or number that proves it.</li><li><strong>Impact</strong>: why it matters for the user or reader.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: what would make it right.</li></ol><p>This is how strong evaluators write, and how they answer interview questions.</p>","secs":[],"words":202},{"id":"bc-your-career-translated","title":"Your career, translated","html":"<p>You&#x27;ve been doing product ownership and evaluation work for years under other names. Learn the right-hand column, because that&#x27;s the language interviewers and job descriptions use.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>What you actually did</th><th>Where (from your CV)</th><th>The formal name for it</th></tr><tr><td>Led UAT sessions and user training for GSK Philippines&#x27; Distributor Management System, through to production go-live</td><td>Netmarks Philippines, 2010–2017</td><td><strong>User acceptance testing</strong>, validating against <strong>acceptance criteria</strong>, <strong>Definition of Done</strong>, <strong>go-live readiness</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Researched and qualified new hardware and software before recommending them to clients</td><td>Netmarks R&amp;D</td><td><strong>Evaluation against criteria</strong>, <strong>trade-off analysis</strong>, <strong>recommendation / decision documents</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Prepared scoping, scopes of work and design documents for implementations</td><td>Netmarks pre-sales and implementations</td><td><strong>Requirements gathering</strong>, <strong>scope definition</strong>, early <strong>specification writing</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Built a lead qualification and follow-up system with shared work/bug logs and human verification; response time went from 6 hours to 7 minutes</td><td>Enterprise lead system, Chatbot Authority (2017–now)</td><td><strong>Problem framing</strong>, <strong>requirements</strong>, <strong>human-in-the-loop design</strong>, <strong>outcome metrics</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Tested every build against real scenarios and edge cases before launch; documented bugs and fixes</td><td>Client builds</td><td><strong>Edge-case analysis</strong>, <strong>QA</strong>, <strong>defect documentation</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Judged a travel client&#x27;s chatbot by transactions, not chat volume (chats → leads → quotes → transactions)</td><td>Pick Up Trip Travel &amp; Tours</td><td><strong>Outcomes vs outputs</strong>, <strong>funnel metrics</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Audited CRM setups for duplicates, broken triggers and leaking stages, then fixed and documented them</td><td>CRM audits</td><td><strong>Data integrity requirements</strong>, <strong>failure-mode analysis</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Built training decks, handover presentations and SOPs for clients and their teams</td><td>Client enablement</td><td><strong>Audience-tailored writing</strong>, <strong>user documentation</strong>, <strong>release communication</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Reviewed and corrected chatbot replies so they were accurate and on-brand</td><td>Client chatbot work</td><td>Close to <strong>content evaluation</strong>; not the same as formal <strong>annotation</strong> or <strong>RLHF</strong>, but it builds the same judgment</td></tr><tr><td>Rated AI output against written rubrics (1 month, 5 tasks so far)</td><td>micro1 CRM Operations Specialist</td><td><strong>Rubric-based evaluation</strong>, <strong>human data work for AI</strong></td></tr></table></div><p><strong>What this means for you.</strong> You&#x27;re strong on the doing. The gap is naming it and explaining your reasoning on paper with explicit criteria. That&#x27;s exactly what Days 4 and 5 train.</p><h4 id=\"from-crm-operations-specialist-to-pm-po-evaluato\">From CRM Operations Specialist to PM/PO evaluator</h4><p>You&#x27;re one month and 5 tasks into your micro1 CRM Operations role, so treat this as a head start, not mastery. That role is evaluation work, and the PM/PO role uses the same craft on different content: product documents instead of CRM workflows. Every task you do now is practice for both.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>What you&#x27;re starting to practise in CRM Operations</th><th>In the PM/PO evaluation role you&#x27;ll…</th><th>What&#x27;s new to learn</th></tr><tr><td>Evaluate AI output on CRM workflows for accuracy against real business practice</td><td>Evaluate AI-written specs, release notes, UX copy and stakeholder updates</td><td>What &quot;good&quot; looks like for each document type (Days 2–3)</td></tr><tr><td>Apply written guidelines and rubrics consistently</td><td>Score on clarity, tone, instruction adherence and usefulness</td><td>Scoring dimensions for writing quality and audience fit (Day 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Flag cases beyond the current guidelines</td><td>Flag fluent writing that doesn&#x27;t answer the question, or exceeds constraints</td><td>Naming writing-specific failure modes (Day 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Give clear, actionable written feedback</td><td>Write detailed rationales for every score</td><td>Rationale structure and precision (Day 5)</td></tr><tr><td>Document pain points and process improvements</td><td>Judge whether content suits executives, engineers or end users</td><td>Audience tailoring (Day 3)</td></tr><tr><td>Work from domain expertise in CRM and sales ops</td><td>Work from domain expertise in product management</td><td>Product vocabulary and frameworks (Days 1–2)</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>The key shift.</strong> In CRM work you judge whether the <em>logic</em> is right. In PM/PO work you judge whether the <em>writing</em> does its job for its reader. Both need the same discipline: explicit criteria, exact evidence, clear impact.</p><h4 id=\"your-computer-engineering-degree-refreshed\">Your Computer Engineering degree, refreshed</h4><p>You studied the roots of all this between 2003 and 2008. You don&#x27;t need to remember the math; you need to recognize the ideas when they come back with new names.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>What you studied</th><th>What it became</th><th>Where it shows up in this course</th></tr><tr><td>Software engineering and the SDLC (requirements → design → build → test → maintain)</td><td>Product discovery and delivery, PRDs, release cycles</td><td>Days 1–2</td></tr><tr><td>Requirements analysis and system specs</td><td>PRDs, user stories, functional and non-functional requirements</td><td>Day 2</td></tr><tr><td>Boolean logic and truth tables (IF, AND, OR, NOT)</td><td>Acceptance criteria, Given/When/Then, rubric rules</td><td>Days 2 and 4</td></tr><tr><td>Testing and debugging (test cases, expected vs actual)</td><td>QA, UAT, evaluating responses against requirements</td><td>Days 2 and 4</td></tr><tr><td>Data structures and file formats</td><td>JSON, schemas, label taxonomies, CRM data models</td><td>Days 4 and 6</td></tr><tr><td>Probability and statistics (mean, variance, sampling)</td><td>Metrics, KPIs, inter-annotator agreement, sampling plans</td><td>Days 1 and 4</td></tr><tr><td>Algorithms and optimization</td><td>How models are trained to maximize a score (reward models, RLHF)</td><td>Day 6</td></tr><tr><td>Signals, feedback and control systems</td><td>Feedback loops: your judgments steer the model, like a controller correcting a system</td><td>Day 6</td></tr><tr><td>Technical writing and documentation</td><td>Specs, release notes, rationales</td><td>Days 3 and 5</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>The feedback-loop idea is the one to hold onto.</strong> In control systems, a sensor measures output, compares it to a target, and the error corrects the system. In RLHF, your evaluation is the sensor, the rubric is the target, and your scores correct the model. Noisy sensors make unstable systems, which is why consistent scoring matters.</p><p><strong>Exercise.</strong> Pick 3 rows above. For each, write 3 sentences describing a real instance using the formal terms, with one number in it (contacts, branches, hours saved, error rate).</p>","secs":[{"id":"from-crm-operations-specialist-to-pm-po-evaluato","t":"From CRM Operations Specialist to PM/PO evaluator"},{"id":"your-computer-engineering-degree-refreshed","t":"Your Computer Engineering degree, refreshed"}],"words":787},{"id":"bc-day-1-product-management-foundations","title":"Day 1: Product management foundations","html":"<p>Product management decides <strong>what</strong> to build and <strong>why</strong>; project and delivery management decide <strong>how and when</strong> it gets done. The PM/PO evaluation role explicitly wants the first and excludes the second, so this distinction is the most important thing you learn today.</p><h4 id=\"1-the-roles-people-confuse\">1. The roles people confuse</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Role</th><th>Owns</th><th>Key question</th><th>Typical outputs</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Product Manager</strong></td><td>The problem, the outcome, the strategy</td><td>&quot;Are we building the right thing?&quot;</td><td>Vision, strategy, PRDs, metrics, prioritized roadmap</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Product Owner</strong> (Scrum)</td><td>The product backlog and its value</td><td>&quot;Is each item clear, valuable and ready?&quot;</td><td>User stories, acceptance criteria, backlog order</td></tr><tr><td>Project Manager</td><td>Scope, schedule, budget of a fixed effort</td><td>&quot;Will we deliver on time and budget?&quot;</td><td>Plans, timelines, risk logs, status reports</td></tr><tr><td>Program Manager</td><td>Several related projects</td><td>&quot;Do the projects add up to the goal?&quot;</td><td>Program plans, dependency maps</td></tr><tr><td>Scrum Master / Delivery Lead</td><td>The team&#x27;s process</td><td>&quot;Is the team working effectively?&quot;</td><td>Ceremonies, impediment removal</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>How to talk about yourself.</strong> Lead with product decisions: &quot;I defined the requirements&quot;, &quot;I decided what the MVP included&quot;, &quot;I wrote the acceptance criteria&quot;, &quot;I judged whether the output met the user&#x27;s need.&quot; Avoid leading with &quot;I managed the timeline&quot; or &quot;I ran the sprints.&quot;</p><h4 id=\"2-problem-space-before-solution-space\">2. Problem space before solution space</h4><ul><li><strong>Problem space</strong>: who has the pain, what it is, how often, what it costs them.</li><li><strong>Solution space</strong>: what you build to remove it.</li><li>Weak specs jump straight to the solution. Strong specs state the problem first, so any reader can judge whether the solution fits.</li><li><strong>Jobs to Be Done (JTBD)</strong>: users &quot;hire&quot; a product to get a job done. Format: <em>When [situation], I want to [motivation], so I can [outcome].</em> Example: &quot;When a lead messages after hours, I want an instant reply, so I don&#x27;t lose them to a competitor.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"3-outcomes-vs-outputs\">3. Outcomes vs outputs</h4><ul><li><strong>Output</strong>: what was shipped (a chatbot, 12 features, a release).</li><li><strong>Outcome</strong>: the change it caused (lead response time down from 6 hours to 2 minutes; 18% more booked calls).</li><li>Good product writing states outcomes. When evaluating, check whether a document confuses the two.</li></ul><h4 id=\"4-users-customers-personas\">4. Users, customers, personas</h4><ul><li><strong>User</strong>: who uses the product. <strong>Customer</strong>: who pays. They&#x27;re often different (the clinic owner pays; the receptionist uses it).</li><li><strong>Persona</strong>: a research-based profile of a user type: goals, frustrations, context.</li><li><strong>Stakeholder</strong>: anyone affected by or influencing the product (sales, support, legal, executives, engineers).</li></ul><h4 id=\"5-discovery-and-delivery\">5. Discovery and delivery</h4><ul><li><strong>Discovery</strong>: learning what to build: interviews, data, prototypes, testing assumptions.</li><li><strong>Delivery</strong>: building and shipping it.</li><li><strong>MVP (Minimum Viable Product)</strong>: the smallest thing that tests whether the solution creates value. Not a cheap version of the full product: a learning tool.</li><li><strong>Assumption / hypothesis</strong>: &quot;We believe [change] will cause [outcome] for [users]. We&#x27;ll know when [measure].&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"6-metrics\">6. Metrics</h4><ul><li><strong>North Star Metric</strong>: the one number that best captures value delivered (e.g., qualified leads booked per week).</li><li><strong>KPI</strong>: a key performance indicator, defined as numerator ÷ denominator over a time window. Example: <em>Lead response rate = leads replied to within 5 minutes ÷ all new leads, per week.</em></li><li><strong>Leading vs lagging</strong>: leading predicts (replies sent); lagging confirms (revenue closed).</li><li><strong>OKRs</strong>: Objective (qualitative goal) + 2–4 Key Results (measurable). Example: <em>O: Make follow-up effortless. KR1: 90% of leads replied to within 5 min. KR2: No-show rate below 15%.</em></li></ul><h4 id=\"7-prioritization-frameworks\">7. Prioritization frameworks</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Framework</th><th>How it works</th><th>When to use</th></tr><tr><td><strong>RICE</strong></td><td>(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort</td><td>Comparing many ideas with some data</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MoSCoW</strong></td><td>Must / Should / Could / Won&#x27;t (this time)</td><td>Scoping a release or MVP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Kano</strong></td><td>Basic needs, performance needs, delighters</td><td>Understanding which features satisfy vs. excite</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Value vs Effort</strong></td><td>2×2 grid: quick wins, big bets, fill-ins, money pits</td><td>Fast team alignment</td></tr></table></div><h4 id=\"day-1-exercises\">Day 1 exercises</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.9346-0\"><span>Write 3 JTBD statements for users of a chatbot funnel you&#x27;ve built.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.9346-1\"><span>Take one client project: write its problem statement, the output you shipped, and the outcome it caused, with a number.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.9346-2\"><span>Define 2 KPIs from your CRM work as numerator ÷ denominator + time window.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.9346-3\"><span>Use MoSCoW to scope an MVP for a &quot;missed-call text-back&quot; feature.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.9346-4\"><span>Out loud, in under 60 seconds: &quot;What&#x27;s the difference between a product manager and a project manager, and which are you?&quot;</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"1-the-roles-people-confuse","t":"1. The roles people confuse"},{"id":"2-problem-space-before-solution-space","t":"2. Problem space before solution space"},{"id":"3-outcomes-vs-outputs","t":"3. Outcomes vs outputs"},{"id":"4-users-customers-personas","t":"4. Users, customers, personas"},{"id":"5-discovery-and-delivery","t":"5. Discovery and delivery"},{"id":"6-metrics","t":"6. Metrics"},{"id":"7-prioritization-frameworks","t":"7. Prioritization frameworks"},{"id":"day-1-exercises","t":"Day 1 exercises"}],"words":626},{"id":"bc-day-2-specifications","title":"Day 2: Specifications","html":"<p>A specification is good when someone who wasn&#x27;t in the room can build and test the right thing from it, without asking you. That&#x27;s the test you&#x27;ll apply when evaluating AI-written specs.</p><h4 id=\"1-the-prd-product-requirements-document\">1. The PRD (Product Requirements Document)</h4><p>A PRD explains what to build and why. A strong one has these parts, roughly in this order:</p><ol><li><strong>Problem statement</strong>: who, what pain, evidence it&#x27;s real.</li><li><strong>Goals and success metrics</strong>: the outcome and how it&#x27;s measured.</li><li><strong>Non-goals / out of scope</strong>: what this deliberately won&#x27;t do. Often the most useful section.</li><li><strong>Users and use cases</strong>: personas, key scenarios.</li><li><strong>Requirements</strong>: functional (what it does) and non-functional (how well: speed, security, reliability, accessibility).</li><li><strong>User flows</strong>: step-by-step paths, including errors.</li><li><strong>Assumptions, constraints, dependencies</strong>: what you&#x27;re taking as true, limits you must respect, what you rely on.</li><li><strong>Risks and open questions</strong>: what could go wrong, what&#x27;s undecided.</li><li><strong>Release plan</strong>: phases, pilot, rollout (high level; not a project schedule).</li></ol><p><strong>Red flags when evaluating a PRD:</strong> no problem statement; metrics without numbers; no out-of-scope section; requirements that are really solutions in disguise; vague words like &quot;fast&quot;, &quot;easy&quot;, &quot;seamless&quot; with no definition.</p><h4 id=\"2-functional-vs-non-functional-requirements\">2. Functional vs non-functional requirements</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Answers</th><th>Example from CRM work</th></tr><tr><td>Functional</td><td>What must the system do?</td><td>&quot;When a form is submitted, create a contact and add it to the New Lead stage.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Non-functional</td><td>How well must it do it?</td><td>&quot;Contact appears in the pipeline within 5 seconds.&quot; &quot;No duplicate contacts with the same email or phone.&quot;</td></tr></table></div><p>Non-functional categories to know: <strong>performance</strong>, <strong>reliability/availability</strong>, <strong>security</strong>, <strong>privacy/compliance</strong>, <strong>scalability</strong>, <strong>usability</strong>, <strong>accessibility</strong>, <strong>maintainability</strong>, <strong>data integrity</strong>.</p><h4 id=\"3-user-stories\">3. User stories</h4><p>Format: <strong>As a</strong> [user], <strong>I want</strong> [capability], <strong>so that</strong> [benefit].</p><p>Example: <em>As a clinic receptionist, I want missed calls to trigger an automatic SMS, so that no caller leaves without a response.</em></p><p><strong>INVEST</strong> checklist for a good story: <strong>I</strong>ndependent, <strong>N</strong>egotiable, <strong>V</strong>aluable, <strong>E</strong>stimable, <strong>S</strong>mall, <strong>T</strong>estable.</p><p><strong>Epic</strong> = a large body of work split into stories. <strong>Backlog</strong> = the ordered list of stories. <strong>Refinement / grooming</strong> = clarifying and splitting stories before work.</p><h4 id=\"4-acceptance-criteria\">4. Acceptance criteria</h4><p>Acceptance criteria define when a story is done. Two common formats:</p><p><strong>Rule-based (checklist):</strong></p><ul><li>SMS is sent within 60 seconds of a missed call.</li><li>SMS is not sent to numbers on the opt-out list.</li><li>SMS is sent only during business hours; after hours, the after-hours message is used.</li></ul><p><strong>Scenario-based (Gherkin: Given / When / Then):</strong></p><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>Given a caller&#x27;s number is not on the opt-out list\nAnd the call is missed during business hours\nWhen 60 seconds pass with no callback\nThen the caller receives the missed-call SMS\nAnd the contact is tagged &quot;missed-call&quot;</code></pre><p><strong>Good criteria are:</strong> testable (pass/fail, no judgment calls), specific (numbers, exact states), complete (cover the unhappy paths), and free of implementation details unless they matter.</p><h4 id=\"5-edge-cases-error-states-ambiguity\">5. Edge cases, error states, ambiguity</h4><ul><li><strong>Happy path</strong>: everything goes right. <strong>Unhappy paths</strong>: everything else.</li><li><strong>Edge case</strong>: valid but unusual input (blank name, international number, duplicate submission, emoji in name).</li><li><strong>Error state</strong>: what the user sees when something fails, and how they recover.</li><li><strong>Ambiguity</strong>: a requirement two people could read differently. Fix by adding an example or a number.</li></ul><p>You already think this way: every &quot;what if the lead types their phone wrong?&quot; you&#x27;ve handled is an edge case.</p><h4 id=\"6-definition-of-ready-definition-of-done\">6. Definition of Ready / Definition of Done</h4><ul><li><strong>Definition of Ready (DoR)</strong>: a story can be started (clear, sized, criteria written, dependencies known).</li><li><strong>Definition of Done (DoD)</strong>: a story is finished (criteria met, tested, documented, deployed). Your UAT sign-offs were DoD checks.</li></ul><h4 id=\"day-2-exercises\">Day 2 exercises</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.13589-0\"><span>Write a one-page PRD for a CRM feature you built (lead routing, duplicate prevention or missed-call text-back), using all 9 sections.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.13589-1\"><span>Write 3 user stories for it and check each against INVEST.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.13589-2\"><span>Write acceptance criteria for one story in both formats, including 2 unhappy paths.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.13589-3\"><span>List 5 edge cases and the expected behavior for each.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.13589-4\"><span>Find one vague word in your PRD (&quot;quickly&quot;, &quot;easily&quot;) and replace it with a number.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"1-the-prd-product-requirements-document","t":"1. The PRD (Product Requirements Document)"},{"id":"2-functional-vs-non-functional-requirements","t":"2. Functional vs non-functional requirements"},{"id":"3-user-stories","t":"3. User stories"},{"id":"4-acceptance-criteria","t":"4. Acceptance criteria"},{"id":"5-edge-cases-error-states-ambiguity","t":"5. Edge cases, error states, ambiguity"},{"id":"6-definition-of-ready-definition-of-done","t":"6. Definition of Ready / Definition of Done"},{"id":"day-2-exercises","t":"Day 2 exercises"}],"words":616},{"id":"bc-day-3-writing-for-every-audience","title":"Day 3: Writing for every audience","html":"<p>The same fact must be written differently for an executive, an engineer and an end user. Judging whether content fits its audience is a core skill in the PM/PO evaluation role.</p><h4 id=\"1-the-three-audiences\">1. The three audiences</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Audience</th><th>Wants</th><th>Reads for</th><th>Good writing</th><th>Bad writing</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Executives</strong></td><td>Decisions, impact, risk</td><td>30 seconds</td><td>Bottom line first, numbers, clear ask</td><td>Technical detail, no recommendation, buried conclusion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Technical stakeholders</strong></td><td>Precision, constraints, edge cases</td><td>Full detail</td><td>Exact behavior, dependencies, failure modes</td><td>Marketing language, vague requirements</td></tr><tr><td><strong>End users</strong></td><td>What changed for <em>me</em>, what to do</td><td>A few seconds</td><td>Plain words, benefit first, one action</td><td>Internal jargon, ticket numbers, blame-shifting</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Same fact, three ways.</strong> <em>Duplicate contact detection now uses phone and email.</em></p><ul><li>Executive: &quot;Duplicate leads dropped 40% this month, so sales stops calling the same person twice. No action needed.&quot;</li><li>Technical: &quot;Dedup now matches on normalized E.164 phone OR lowercased email. Conflicts merge into the oldest record; merge events are logged.&quot;</li><li>End user: &quot;You&#x27;ll no longer see the same customer listed twice.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"2-writing-frameworks-to-know-by-name\">2. Writing frameworks to know by name</h4><ul><li><strong>BLUF (Bottom Line Up Front)</strong>: conclusion or ask in the first sentence.</li><li><strong>Pyramid Principle</strong> (Barbara Minto): main point first, then grouped supporting arguments, then details.</li><li><strong>SCQA</strong>: Situation, Complication, Question, Answer. Good for proposals.</li><li><strong>Inverted pyramid</strong>: most important information first, details later. Used in release notes and news.</li><li><strong>Plain language</strong>: short sentences, common words, active voice, one idea per sentence.</li></ul><h4 id=\"3-release-notes\">3. Release notes</h4><p>Release notes tell users what changed and why they should care.</p><ul><li><strong>Structure</strong>: version/date → highlights → new → improved → fixed → known issues / action required.</li><li><strong>Each item</strong>: user benefit first, then what changed. &quot;Find any contact in seconds: search now matches partial phone numbers.&quot; Not: &quot;Refactored search index (JIRA-4821).&quot;</li><li><strong>Changelog</strong> (technical, complete) vs <strong>release notes</strong> (user-facing, curated).</li><li><strong>Red flags</strong>: internal ticket IDs, jargon, no benefit, breaking changes not flagged, missing &quot;what you need to do&quot;.</li></ul><h4 id=\"4-user-facing-copy-ux-writing-microcopy\">4. User-facing copy (UX writing / microcopy)</h4><p>Buttons, error messages, empty states, tooltips, onboarding.</p><ul><li><strong>Buttons</strong>: verb + object (&quot;Send invoice&quot;, not &quot;Submit&quot;).</li><li><strong>Error messages</strong>: what happened + why (if useful) + how to fix. &quot;That phone number is missing a digit. Check it and try again.&quot; Never blame the user; avoid &quot;Error 403&quot; alone.</li><li><strong>Empty states</strong>: explain what goes here and the first action.</li><li><strong>Tone</strong>: consistent with the brand voice; adjusted to the moment (calm in errors, warm in success).</li><li><strong>Constraints matter</strong>: character limits, reading level, localization.</li></ul><h4 id=\"5-stakeholder-updates\">5. Stakeholder updates</h4><ul><li><strong>Structure</strong>: status in one line (on track / at risk / off track) → what changed since last update → metrics → risks and blockers → decisions or help needed → next steps.</li><li><strong>Good</strong>: honest status, numbers, explicit asks with owner and date.</li><li><strong>Bad</strong>: activity lists without outcomes (&quot;we had 5 meetings&quot;), hiding bad news, no ask.</li></ul><h4 id=\"6-decision-documents\">6. Decision documents</h4><p>A decision doc records what was decided, why, and what was rejected.</p><ul><li><strong>Common formats</strong>: one-pager, <strong>RFC</strong> (Request for Comments), <strong>ADR</strong> (Architecture Decision Record), <strong>decision log</strong>.</li><li><strong>Structure</strong>: context → decision → options considered (with pros/cons) → rationale → trade-offs accepted → consequences → owner and date.</li><li><strong>Red flags</strong>: no alternatives considered; rationale that&#x27;s just preference (&quot;it&#x27;s better&quot;); no trade-offs named; the decision isn&#x27;t actually stated.</li></ul><h4 id=\"day-3-exercises\">Day 3 exercises</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.17568-0\"><span>Write one change from your work three ways: executive, technical, end user.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.17568-1\"><span>Write release notes for 3 changes to a funnel or bot, benefit first.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.17568-2\"><span>Rewrite 3 error messages from a tool you use so they explain how to fix the problem.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.17568-3\"><span>Write a BLUF stakeholder update for a client project, ending with a clear ask.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.17568-4\"><span>Write a decision doc for why you chose one automation tool over another, with 2 rejected options.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"1-the-three-audiences","t":"1. The three audiences"},{"id":"2-writing-frameworks-to-know-by-name","t":"2. Writing frameworks to know by name"},{"id":"3-release-notes","t":"3. Release notes"},{"id":"4-user-facing-copy-ux-writing-microcopy","t":"4. User-facing copy (UX writing / microcopy)"},{"id":"5-stakeholder-updates","t":"5. Stakeholder updates"},{"id":"6-decision-documents","t":"6. Decision documents"},{"id":"day-3-exercises","t":"Day 3 exercises"}],"words":559},{"id":"bc-day-4-evaluating-content","title":"Day 4: Evaluating content","html":"<p>Evaluation means judging a response against explicit criteria, consistently, with evidence. Your score is only as good as the rule behind it: if you can&#x27;t name the criterion, you&#x27;re giving an opinion, not an evaluation.</p><h4 id=\"1-the-core-scoring-dimensions\">1. The core scoring dimensions</h4><p>These map directly to the PM/PO role: clarity, tone, adherence to instructions, usefulness for the audience, plus accuracy and constraint compliance.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>The question you ask</th><th>Evidence you look for</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Instruction following</strong></td><td>Did it do exactly what the prompt asked?</td><td>Each requirement in the prompt, ticked or missed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Constraint compliance</strong></td><td>Did it respect limits?</td><td>Length, format, number of items, required sections, banned content</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accuracy</strong></td><td>Is everything true and internally consistent?</td><td>Wrong facts, numbers that don&#x27;t add up, contradictions between sections</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Completeness</strong></td><td>Is anything required missing?</td><td>Missing sections, unanswered parts of a multi-part prompt</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Usefulness / helpfulness</strong></td><td>Could the intended reader act on it?</td><td>Concrete next steps, specifics vs. generic filler</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Audience fit</strong></td><td>Is it right for this reader?</td><td>Level of detail, jargon, length, what&#x27;s emphasized</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clarity</strong></td><td>Is it easy to understand on first read?</td><td>Structure, sentence length, ambiguity, buried point</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tone</strong></td><td>Is the voice appropriate?</td><td>Formal vs casual, confident vs hedging, empathetic in bad news</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Concision</strong></td><td>Is every sentence earning its place?</td><td>Repetition, padding, throat-clearing intros</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Order of importance (a common, sensible default):</strong> a response that ignores the instructions or states something false fails, however well written. Check instruction following and accuracy first, then usefulness and audience fit, then clarity, tone and concision. Always follow the project&#x27;s own guidelines if they set a different priority.</p><h4 id=\"2-rubrics-and-rating-scales\">2. Rubrics and rating scales</h4><ul><li><strong>Rubric</strong>: a scoring guide with defined criteria and level descriptions.</li><li><strong>Likert scale</strong>: e.g., 1–5 or 1–7. Each point needs a written anchor, or scores drift.</li><li><strong>Anchors</strong>: the description of each level. Example for instruction following: <em>5 = all requirements met; 4 = one minor requirement missed; 3 = one major or several minor missed; 2 = most missed; 1 = ignores the prompt.</em></li><li><strong>Analytic vs holistic</strong>: analytic scores each dimension separately; holistic gives one overall score. Many projects use both.</li><li><strong>Pass/fail threshold</strong>: the cut-off that decides acceptance.</li></ul><h4 id=\"3-pairwise-comparison-side-by-side\">3. Pairwise comparison (side-by-side)</h4><p>You often compare Response A and Response B and pick the better one, sometimes with a strength (&quot;A is much better&quot;, &quot;slightly better&quot;, &quot;about the same&quot;).</p><ul><li>Judge each against the prompt first, then compare. Don&#x27;t let one response set the standard for the other.</li><li>A shorter response that fully meets the prompt beats a longer one that misses a requirement.</li><li>&quot;About the same&quot; is valid only when you can name why they&#x27;re equal.</li></ul><h4 id=\"4-failure-modes-to-catch\">4. Failure modes to catch</h4><p>These are named explicitly in the PM/PO scope: fluent writing that fails, complete responses unsuitable for users, inaccuracies, constraint breaches.</p><ul><li><strong>Fluent but wrong</strong>: reads beautifully, answers a different question.</li><li><strong>Complete but unusable</strong>: every section present, but too technical, too long or wrong for the reader.</li><li><strong>Hallucination</strong>: invented facts, features, numbers or sources.</li><li><strong>Constraint violation</strong>: 250 words when the limit was 150; 5 bullets when 3 were asked; a missing required section.</li><li><strong>Scope creep</strong>: adds content the prompt didn&#x27;t ask for (a roadmap in a release note).</li><li><strong>Internal inconsistency</strong>: says &quot;launches Monday&quot; in one place and &quot;next week&quot; in another.</li><li><strong>Generic filler</strong>: could apply to any product (&quot;improves user experience&quot;).</li><li><strong>Wrong tone</strong>: cheerful in an outage notice; casual in an exec update.</li><li><strong>Sycophancy</strong>: agreeing with a flawed premise in the prompt instead of correcting it.</li></ul><h4 id=\"5-consistency-and-calibration\">5. Consistency and calibration</h4><ul><li><strong>Calibration</strong>: aligning with other reviewers on what each score means, usually by scoring the same examples and discussing differences.</li><li><strong>Gold set / golden examples</strong>: responses with agreed correct scores, used to check accuracy.</li><li><strong>Inter-annotator agreement (IAA)</strong>: how often reviewers give the same score. Low IAA means the guideline is unclear or reviewers drift.</li><li><strong>Drift</strong>: your standard slowly changing over a long session. Re-read the anchors regularly.</li><li><strong>Bias to watch</strong>: preferring longer responses (<strong>length bias</strong>), the first one you read (<strong>order bias</strong>), or your own style.</li><li><strong>Escalate, don&#x27;t guess</strong>: when a case isn&#x27;t covered by the guidelines, flag it with a short note: the ambiguity, the rule that&#x27;s missing, and the decision you need.</li></ul><h4 id=\"6-a-repeatable-evaluation-routine\">6. A repeatable evaluation routine</h4><ol><li>Read the prompt. List every explicit requirement and constraint (count them).</li><li>Identify the audience and purpose.</li><li>Read the response once for the overall picture.</li><li>Check each requirement: met, partly met, missed. Note the exact words as evidence.</li><li>Check accuracy and consistency.</li><li>Judge usefulness, audience fit, clarity, tone.</li><li>Score each dimension against the anchors.</li><li>Write the rationale (Day 5).</li></ol><h4 id=\"day-4-exercises\">Day 4 exercises</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.22966-0\"><span>Write a 1–5 rubric with anchors for &quot;audience fit&quot;.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.22966-1\"><span>Ask an AI assistant to write release notes with 3 constraints (e.g., under 120 words, exactly 3 bullets, include an action-required line). Evaluate it with the routine above.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.22966-2\"><span>Ask for two versions of an exec update; compare them side by side and pick one with a reason.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.22966-3\"><span>Find and name 3 failure modes in AI-written content you&#x27;ve seen at work.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.22966-4\"><span>Out loud: &quot;How do you keep your scoring consistent across 200 responses?&quot;</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"1-the-core-scoring-dimensions","t":"1. The core scoring dimensions"},{"id":"2-rubrics-and-rating-scales","t":"2. Rubrics and rating scales"},{"id":"3-pairwise-comparison-side-by-side","t":"3. Pairwise comparison (side-by-side)"},{"id":"4-failure-modes-to-catch","t":"4. Failure modes to catch"},{"id":"5-consistency-and-calibration","t":"5. Consistency and calibration"},{"id":"6-a-repeatable-evaluation-routine","t":"6. A repeatable evaluation routine"},{"id":"day-4-exercises","t":"Day 4 exercises"}],"words":768},{"id":"bc-day-5-writing-rationales-that-hold-up","title":"Day 5: Writing rationales that hold up","html":"<p>A rationale is the written proof behind your score. A reviewer who never saw the response should be able to read it and agree, or at least see exactly why you decided what you did.</p><h4 id=\"1-the-rationale-formula\">1. The rationale formula</h4><p><strong>Verdict → Criterion → Evidence (quoted) → Impact → Fix</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Verdict</strong>: the score or preference, stated first.</li><li><strong>Criterion</strong>: which rule or prompt requirement you&#x27;re applying.</li><li><strong>Evidence</strong>: the exact words, quoted, or the exact count (&quot;187 words vs. a 150-word limit&quot;).</li><li><strong>Impact</strong>: why it matters for the intended reader.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: what would have earned a higher score (optional but valued).</li></ul><h4 id=\"2-weak-vs-strong\">2. Weak vs strong</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Weak</th><th>Strong</th></tr><tr><td>&quot;Response A is better and clearer.&quot;</td><td>&quot;A is better: it meets all 4 prompt requirements; B misses the &#x27;action required&#x27; line, so users won&#x27;t know to update their settings.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;The tone is off.&quot;</td><td>&quot;Tone is too casual for an executive audience: &#x27;Heads up, folks, small hiccup!&#x27; trivializes a 3-hour outage.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;It&#x27;s too long.&quot;</td><td>&quot;Violates the length constraint: 214 words against a 150-word limit. The &#x27;Background&#x27; paragraph could be cut without losing anything the reader needs.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Some info is wrong.&quot;</td><td>&quot;Inconsistent: the summary says &#x27;launches March 3&#x27; but the timeline table says March 10.&quot;</td></tr></table></div><h4 id=\"3-worked-example\">3. Worked example</h4><p><strong>Prompt:</strong> <em>Write release notes for end users (non-technical) about this change: search now matches partial phone numbers, and the old &#x27;Advanced search&#x27; menu is removed. Under 100 words. Must include what users need to do differently.</em></p><p><strong>Response A:</strong></p><blockquote><p><strong>Faster search is here!</strong> You can now find any contact by typing just part of their phone number, for example the last 4 digits. We&#x27;ve also simplified things by removing the Advanced search menu. Everything it did is now in the main search bar, so just type and go.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Response B:</strong></p><blockquote><p><strong>Release 4.2.1 (SRCH-882, SRCH-901).</strong> Implemented partial-match tokenization on the phone field using n-gram indexing, improving recall. Deprecated the legacy Advanced Search modal (AdvSearchV1) to reduce UI complexity and maintenance overhead. Performance improved by approximately 35% on large datasets.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Strong rationale:</strong></p><blockquote><p><strong>A is much better than B.</strong></p><p><strong>Audience fit:</strong> The prompt targets non-technical end users. A uses plain language and leads with the benefit (&quot;find any contact by typing just part of their phone number&quot;). B uses internal jargon (&quot;n-gram indexing&quot;, &quot;tokenization&quot;, &quot;AdvSearchV1&quot;) and ticket IDs a user can&#x27;t act on.</p><p><strong>Instruction following:</strong> Both stay under 100 words (A: 49, B: 38). The prompt requires &quot;what users need to do differently.&quot; A states it (&quot;Everything it did is now in the main search bar, so just type and go&quot;). B says the menu was deprecated but never tells users what to do instead, so it fails this requirement.</p><p><strong>Accuracy:</strong> B adds &quot;Performance improved by approximately 35%&quot;, which isn&#x27;t in the source change. It&#x27;s a possible hallucination and goes beyond the prompt.</p><p><strong>Fix for B:</strong> Remove ticket IDs and technical terms, lead with the user benefit, and add one sentence telling users where Advanced search features now live.</p></blockquote><p>Notice: verdict first, each paragraph names a criterion, every claim has quoted evidence or a count, impact is clear, and there&#x27;s a fix.</p><h4 id=\"4-rationale-habits\">4. Rationale habits</h4><ul><li><strong>Quote, don&#x27;t paraphrase.</strong> Put the exact words in quotes.</li><li><strong>Count.</strong> Words, bullets, requirements met (&quot;3 of 4&quot;).</li><li><strong>One criterion per paragraph or sentence.</strong> Easy to scan, easy to check.</li><li><strong>Neutral and professional.</strong> Describe the content, never the writer (&quot;the response omits&quot;, not &quot;the AI was lazy&quot;).</li><li><strong>Credit what works.</strong> Name strengths too; it shows balanced judgment and helps calibration.</li><li><strong>Separate severity.</strong> A missed requirement or false fact outweighs a slightly awkward sentence. Say which issues drove the score.</li><li><strong>Be concise.</strong> Long isn&#x27;t thorough. Specific is thorough.</li></ul><h4 id=\"5-escalation-notes-cases-beyond-the-guidelines\">5. Escalation notes (cases beyond the guidelines)</h4><p>One short, structured note:</p><blockquote><p><strong>Ambiguity:</strong> The prompt asks for &quot;a short update&quot; with no length limit. <strong>Missing rule:</strong> Guidelines don&#x27;t define &quot;short&quot;. <strong>Decision needed:</strong> Should 150+ words count as a constraint failure? <strong>What I did meanwhile:</strong> Scored it without penalty and flagged it.</p></blockquote><h4 id=\"day-5-exercises\">Day 5 exercises</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.27874-0\"><span>Rewrite the 4 weak rationales in the table above in your own words, using the formula.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.27874-1\"><span>Ask an AI assistant for two stakeholder updates on the same project; write a full pairwise rationale.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.27874-2\"><span>Write one escalation note for an ambiguous prompt you invent.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.27874-3\"><span>Take a past client report you wrote; write a rationale scoring your own work on clarity and audience fit.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.27874-4\"><span>Time yourself: one full rationale in under 10 minutes.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"1-the-rationale-formula","t":"1. The rationale formula"},{"id":"2-weak-vs-strong","t":"2. Weak vs strong"},{"id":"3-worked-example","t":"3. Worked example"},{"id":"4-rationale-habits","t":"4. Rationale habits"},{"id":"5-escalation-notes-cases-beyond-the-guidelines","t":"5. Escalation notes (cases beyond the guidelines)"},{"id":"day-5-exercises","t":"Day 5 exercises"}],"words":694},{"id":"bc-day-6-how-ai-models-learn-from-your-judgments","title":"Day 6: How AI models learn from your judgments","html":"<p>Your scores and rationales become training signal: they teach models what &quot;good&quot; looks like. Understanding the pipeline helps you see why consistency and precision matter so much, and gives you the vocabulary to discuss it.</p><h4 id=\"1-how-a-language-model-is-built-in-four-stages\">1. How a language model is built, in four stages</h4><ol><li><strong>Pre-training</strong>: the model learns language by predicting the next word (technically, the next <strong>token</strong>) across huge amounts of text. It becomes fluent, but not necessarily helpful or accurate.</li><li><strong>Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)</strong>: experts write example prompts and ideal responses (<strong>demonstrations</strong>). The model learns to imitate them.</li><li><strong>Preference learning</strong>: experts compare responses and say which is better (<strong>preference data</strong>). This teaches the model what people prefer.</li><li><strong>Evaluation</strong>: the model is tested against benchmarks and expert rubrics to measure progress and catch regressions.</li></ol><p>Your PM/PO and CRM evaluation work fits stages 2–4: writing good examples, ranking responses, and scoring against rubrics.</p><h4 id=\"2-rlhf-in-plain-words\">2. RLHF in plain words</h4><p><strong>Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF):</strong></p><ol><li>The model produces several responses to the same prompt.</li><li>Humans rank or compare them (your pairwise judgments).</li><li>Those comparisons train a <strong>reward model</strong>: a separate model that predicts which response humans would prefer.</li><li>The main model is then optimized to produce responses the reward model scores highly.</li></ol><p>Related terms you may hear:</p><ul><li><strong>DPO (Direct Preference Optimization)</strong>: learns from preference pairs directly, without a separate reward model.</li><li><strong>RLAIF</strong>: feedback from an AI model instead of humans, often guided by human-written principles.</li><li><strong>Reward hacking</strong>: the model learns to please the scorer without truly improving (e.g., getting longer because long answers were preferred). This is why length bias in evaluators is harmful.</li></ul><h4 id=\"3-why-your-consistency-matters\">3. Why your consistency matters</h4><ul><li>Inconsistent scores are <strong>noise</strong>: the model can&#x27;t learn a clear standard from them.</li><li>Biased scores (always preferring longer, more confident or more formal text) get <strong>amplified</strong>: the model learns the bias.</li><li>Rationales let QA reviewers check your reasoning, settle disagreements and improve guidelines. In some projects they&#x27;re also used as training data.</li><li><strong>Quality and velocity</strong> are both measured: accurate work done at a steady pace.</li></ul><h4 id=\"4-evaluation-vocabulary\">4. Evaluation vocabulary</h4><ul><li><strong>Eval / evaluation set</strong>: a fixed set of prompts used to measure a model.</li><li><strong>Benchmark</strong>: a standard public eval used to compare models.</li><li><strong>Rubric-based grading</strong>: scoring against defined criteria, which is your core task.</li><li><strong>Contextual / domain evaluation</strong>: judging performance in a specific setting (CRM workflows, product documents, legal, medical).</li><li><strong>Red teaming</strong>: deliberately trying to make a model fail or produce harmful output, to find weaknesses.</li><li><strong>Regression</strong>: a new model version getting worse at something the old one did well.</li><li><strong>Ground truth</strong>: the agreed correct answer.</li><li><strong>Edge-case coverage</strong>: making sure evals include unusual but realistic situations.</li></ul><h4 id=\"5-annotation-vocabulary\">5. Annotation vocabulary</h4><ul><li><strong>Annotation / labeling</strong>: adding structured judgments to data (a score, a category, a tag, a correction).</li><li><strong>Annotation guidelines</strong>: the project&#x27;s rulebook. Always the highest authority; follow it over your personal preference.</li><li><strong>Label taxonomy</strong>: the fixed set of allowed labels and their definitions.</li><li><strong>Gold set</strong>: pre-scored items used to check annotator accuracy.</li><li><strong>Inter-annotator agreement (IAA)</strong>: how consistently different annotators label the same items.</li><li><strong>Adjudication</strong>: a senior reviewer settling disagreements.</li><li><strong>Edge-case log</strong>: a running list of tricky cases and how they were decided, so everyone decides them the same way next time.</li></ul><h4 id=\"6-common-model-failure-patterns\">6. Common model failure patterns</h4><ul><li><strong>Hallucination</strong>: confident, fluent, false.</li><li><strong>Instruction drift</strong>: ignores part of a multi-part prompt, usually the last part.</li><li><strong>Over-refusal</strong>: declines a reasonable request.</li><li><strong>Sycophancy</strong>: agrees with the user&#x27;s wrong assumption.</li><li><strong>Verbosity</strong>: pads answers; restates the question.</li><li><strong>Format errors</strong>: wrong structure, broken tables, missing required sections.</li></ul><h4 id=\"day-6-exercises\">Day 6 exercises</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.32310-0\"><span>Explain RLHF out loud in 60 seconds to a non-technical friend.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.32310-1\"><span>Write one demonstration: a prompt plus the ideal response, for a stakeholder update.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.32310-2\"><span>Ask an AI assistant a CRM question with a false premise (&quot;Why does HubSpot delete contacts after 30 days?&quot;). Note whether it corrects you or goes along with it.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.32310-3\"><span>Start your own edge-case log: 5 tricky situations from your CRM tasks and how you&#x27;d decide them.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.32310-4\"><span>Out loud: &quot;Why do evaluators need to avoid length bias?&quot;</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"1-how-a-language-model-is-built-in-four-stages","t":"1. How a language model is built, in four stages"},{"id":"2-rlhf-in-plain-words","t":"2. RLHF in plain words"},{"id":"3-why-your-consistency-matters","t":"3. Why your consistency matters"},{"id":"4-evaluation-vocabulary","t":"4. Evaluation vocabulary"},{"id":"5-annotation-vocabulary","t":"5. Annotation vocabulary"},{"id":"6-common-model-failure-patterns","t":"6. Common model failure patterns"},{"id":"day-6-exercises","t":"Day 6 exercises"}],"words":623},{"id":"bc-day-7-interview-readiness","title":"Day 7: Interview readiness","html":"<p>Today you turn the week into answers. For the PM/PO evaluation role, expect questions on the job description&#x27;s skills: product ownership, specification writing, content evaluation, written communication, critical analysis and audience tailoring. Every answer uses real examples from your work, with numbers.</p><h4 id=\"1-be-honest-about-your-positioning\">1. Be honest about your positioning</h4><p>The job prefers &quot;professional experience as a Product Manager or certified Product Owner.&quot; Your honest version: as a founder and consultant you <strong>owned the product decisions</strong> for client systems (what to build, the requirements, acceptance, and whether it served users), and you&#x27;ve done <strong>UAT and rubric-based evaluation</strong>. Say that clearly; don&#x27;t claim a PM job title you didn&#x27;t hold.</p><ul><li>Lead with product ownership and evaluation, never with timelines, sprints or managing people (the role excludes those).</li><li>If you want the credential, the <strong>Professional Scrum Product Owner (PSPO I)</strong> from Scrum.org and the <strong>Certified Scrum Product Owner (CSPO)</strong> from Scrum Alliance are the widely recognized ones.</li></ul><p><strong>Honest boundaries (keep these in every answer):</strong></p><ul><li>You&#x27;ve owned product decisions in client and consulting work; you haven&#x27;t held a PM or PO job title.</li><li>Your micro1 evaluation experience is real but new: one month, 5 tasks.</li><li>Reviewing chatbot replies or tagging CRM records is related experience, not formal ML annotation or RLHF.</li><li>Name the tools you&#x27;ve genuinely used on real projects; don&#x27;t list tools you&#x27;ve only touched to sound broad.</li><li>Interviewers probe whatever you claim, so claim only what you can explain in depth.</li></ul><h4 id=\"2-your-60-second-introduction-draft-make-it-your\">2. Your 60-second introduction (draft, make it yours)</h4><blockquote><p>I&#x27;m a systems and automation specialist with 15+ years across IT, systems integration and automation. I started in computer engineering, then enterprise systems integration, where I led UAT and user training for a distributor management system rollout through go-live. Since 2017 I&#x27;ve run my own consultancy building CRM, chatbot and automation systems for small businesses. In that work I own the product side: I turn a client&#x27;s problem into requirements, decide what goes into the first version, write the acceptance criteria and judge whether the result actually serves their users. This past month I&#x27;ve started rubric-based evaluation of AI output as a CRM Operations Specialist. I&#x27;m applying because evaluating specs and product writing is exactly where product judgment and evaluation meet.</p></blockquote><h4 id=\"3-prepare-5-stories-star-specific-with-numbers\">3. Prepare 5 stories (STAR, specific, with numbers)</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Skill it proves</th><th>Your story (from your CV)</th><th>Make sure to include</th></tr><tr><td>Specification writing / acceptance</td><td>GSK Philippines DMS rollout: you led UAT and user training through go-live</td><td>What &quot;passed&quot; meant (the criteria you tested against), one defect you caught, how sign-off worked</td></tr><tr><td>Product ownership / outcomes</td><td>Enterprise lead system: qualification and follow-up automation with work/bug logs and human verification</td><td>The problem; what you put in version 1 and what you left out; 6 hrs → 7 min response, 468 opt-ins, 11.75 hrs/week saved</td></tr><tr><td>Content / output evaluation</td><td>Testing client builds and chatbot replies against real scenarios and edge cases before launch</td><td>One specific wrong or off-brand output, why it failed, how you documented and fixed it</td></tr><tr><td>Audience tailoring</td><td>Training decks, handover presentations and SOPs for client teams; classroom training for sales and technical engineers at Netmarks</td><td>How the same system was explained differently to owners vs. staff vs. engineers</td></tr><tr><td>Critical analysis / decisions</td><td>Netmarks R&amp;D: qualifying hardware and software before recommending it to clients; or Pick Up Trip, judging success by transactions, not chats</td><td>The criteria you used, options rejected, the trade-off, the result</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Use numbers you can explain.</strong> For every figure, know how it was measured and over what period. If a number is a client-reported result, say so (&quot;the client reported…&quot;).</p><p><strong>STAR reminder:</strong> Situation (brief) → Task → <strong>Action (60%, using &quot;I&quot;)</strong> → Result (with a number).</p><h4 id=\"4-practice-questions\">4. Practice questions</h4><p>Answer each out loud in under 2 minutes, then write it down.</p><ol><li>Walk me through a specification you wrote. What made it good?</li><li>What&#x27;s the difference between a PRD and a user story? When would you use each?</li><li>Write acceptance criteria for a password reset feature. (Practice on paper: include unhappy paths.)</li><li>How would you judge whether release notes are right for end users vs. engineers?</li><li>Here are two stakeholder updates. Which is better, and why? (Use the rationale formula.)</li><li>How do you tell when a well-written response doesn&#x27;t actually answer the prompt?</li><li>A response is accurate but 40% over the word limit. How do you score it?</li><li>How do you stay consistent when scoring hundreds of responses?</li><li>What do you do when the guidelines don&#x27;t cover a case?</li><li>What makes an executive update effective?</li></ol><p><strong>Model answer outline for #6:</strong> &quot;I list every requirement in the prompt before I read the response, then tick each one with quoted evidence. Fluent writing can hide a missed requirement, so I check what was asked, not how it sounds. For example, release notes can read well but never tell users what to do differently. If the prompt required that, it&#x27;s a failed requirement, whatever the quality of the prose.&quot;</p><h4 id=\"5-delivery-tips\">5. Delivery tips</h4><ul><li>Use the answer shape: <strong>verdict → rule → evidence → impact → fix.</strong></li><li>Say numbers: words, %, hours, records, requirements met.</li><li>Use the vocabulary naturally: acceptance criteria, edge cases, audience fit, instruction following, constraints, rubric.</li><li>If you need a moment: &quot;Let me think about that for a second.&quot;</li><li>Answer multi-part questions in order: &quot;First… second…&quot;</li><li>Speak at your natural pace; clarity beats perfect English.</li></ul><h4 id=\"6-final-day-checklist\">6. Final-day checklist</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.38885-0\"><span>Read the job description again; list its 10 required skills and one story for each.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.38885-1\"><span>Rehearse the 60-second intro 3 times.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.38885-2\"><span>Answer all 10 practice questions out loud.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.38885-3\"><span>Re-read the glossary; cover the right column and define each term.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.38885-4\"><span>Do one full evaluation exercise (Day 4 routine + Day 5 rationale) in under 15 minutes.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bc-.38885-5\"><span>Set up: quiet room, good light, stable internet, one screen, phone away.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"1-be-honest-about-your-positioning","t":"1. Be honest about your positioning"},{"id":"2-your-60-second-introduction-draft-make-it-your","t":"2. Your 60-second introduction (draft, make it yours)"},{"id":"3-prepare-5-stories-star-specific-with-numbers","t":"3. Prepare 5 stories (STAR, specific, with numbers)"},{"id":"4-practice-questions","t":"4. Practice questions"},{"id":"5-delivery-tips","t":"5. Delivery tips"},{"id":"6-final-day-checklist","t":"6. Final-day checklist"}],"words":910},{"id":"bc-glossary","title":"Glossary","html":"<p>Cover the right column and test yourself daily. Grouped by topic, alphabetical within each group.</p><p><strong>Product management</strong></p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Backlog</td><td>The ordered list of work items (stories) for a product</td></tr><tr><td>Discovery</td><td>Learning what to build before building it</td></tr><tr><td>Epic</td><td>A large piece of work split into smaller stories</td></tr><tr><td>Jobs to Be Done (JTBD)</td><td>The progress a user is trying to make; &quot;When… I want to… so I can…&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>KPI</td><td>Key performance indicator: a defined metric (numerator ÷ denominator, over time)</td></tr><tr><td>Leading / lagging indicator</td><td>Leading predicts results; lagging confirms them afterward</td></tr><tr><td>MoSCoW</td><td>Must / Should / Could / Won&#x27;t prioritization</td></tr><tr><td>MVP</td><td>Minimum viable product: the smallest thing that tests whether a solution creates value</td></tr><tr><td>North Star Metric</td><td>The single metric that best captures value delivered to users</td></tr><tr><td>OKR</td><td>Objective + measurable Key Results</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome vs output</td><td>Outcome = change caused; output = thing shipped</td></tr><tr><td>Persona</td><td>A research-based profile of a user type</td></tr><tr><td>Product Owner</td><td>Owns the backlog and its value, usually in Scrum</td></tr><tr><td>RICE</td><td>(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort</td></tr><tr><td>Stakeholder</td><td>Anyone affected by or influencing the product</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Specifications</strong></p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Acceptance criteria</td><td>Testable conditions a story must meet to be done</td></tr><tr><td>Assumption</td><td>Something treated as true without proof yet</td></tr><tr><td>Constraint</td><td>A limit the solution must respect (budget, platform, law)</td></tr><tr><td>Definition of Done (DoD)</td><td>The shared checklist for when work is finished</td></tr><tr><td>Definition of Ready (DoR)</td><td>The checklist for when work can start</td></tr><tr><td>Dependency</td><td>Something the work relies on (another team, an API)</td></tr><tr><td>Edge case</td><td>A valid but unusual situation</td></tr><tr><td>Functional requirement</td><td>What the system must do</td></tr><tr><td>Gherkin / Given-When-Then</td><td>Scenario format for acceptance criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Happy / unhappy path</td><td>Everything goes right / something goes wrong</td></tr><tr><td>INVEST</td><td>Good story test: Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable</td></tr><tr><td>Non-functional requirement</td><td>How well it must work: speed, security, reliability, accessibility</td></tr><tr><td>Non-goals / out of scope</td><td>What the work deliberately won&#x27;t do</td></tr><tr><td>PRD</td><td>Product Requirements Document: what to build and why</td></tr><tr><td>UAT</td><td>User acceptance testing: users confirm it meets requirements</td></tr><tr><td>User story</td><td>&quot;As a… I want… so that…&quot;</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Product writing</strong></p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr><tr><td>ADR</td><td>Architecture Decision Record: a short record of a technical decision</td></tr><tr><td>BLUF</td><td>Bottom line up front</td></tr><tr><td>Changelog</td><td>Complete technical list of changes</td></tr><tr><td>Microcopy</td><td>Small UI text: buttons, errors, tooltips</td></tr><tr><td>Plain language</td><td>Short sentences, common words, active voice</td></tr><tr><td>Pyramid Principle</td><td>Main point first, then grouped support</td></tr><tr><td>Release notes</td><td>User-facing summary of what changed and why it matters</td></tr><tr><td>RFC</td><td>Request for Comments: a proposal shared for feedback</td></tr><tr><td>SCQA</td><td>Situation, Complication, Question, Answer</td></tr><tr><td>Tone / voice</td><td>Voice = consistent personality; tone = how it adapts to the moment</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Evaluation and AI</strong></p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Adjudication</td><td>A senior reviewer resolving disagreements</td></tr><tr><td>Anchor</td><td>The written description of each score level</td></tr><tr><td>Annotation / labeling</td><td>Adding structured judgments to data</td></tr><tr><td>Calibration</td><td>Aligning reviewers on what each score means</td></tr><tr><td>DPO</td><td>Direct Preference Optimization: training on preference pairs without a reward model</td></tr><tr><td>Gold set</td><td>Pre-scored items used to check accuracy</td></tr><tr><td>Hallucination</td><td>Confident, fluent, false content</td></tr><tr><td>Holistic vs analytic scoring</td><td>One overall score vs one per dimension</td></tr><tr><td>Instruction following</td><td>Doing exactly what the prompt asked</td></tr><tr><td>Inter-annotator agreement (IAA)</td><td>How often different reviewers give the same judgment</td></tr><tr><td>Length bias</td><td>Unfairly preferring longer responses</td></tr><tr><td>Likert scale</td><td>A rating scale like 1–5</td></tr><tr><td>Pairwise comparison</td><td>Choosing the better of two responses</td></tr><tr><td>Preference data</td><td>Records of which response humans preferred</td></tr><tr><td>Rationale</td><td>Written justification for a score</td></tr><tr><td>Red teaming</td><td>Deliberately trying to make a model fail</td></tr><tr><td>Reward hacking</td><td>A model pleasing the scorer without truly improving</td></tr><tr><td>Reward model</td><td>A model trained to predict human preferences</td></tr><tr><td>RLHF</td><td>Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback</td></tr><tr><td>Rubric</td><td>Scoring guide with criteria and level descriptions</td></tr><tr><td>SFT</td><td>Supervised fine-tuning on expert-written examples</td></tr><tr><td>Sycophancy</td><td>Agreeing with a user&#x27;s wrong premise</td></tr><tr><td>Token</td><td>The unit of text a model reads and writes (a word or part of one)</td></tr></table></div>","secs":[],"words":492},{"id":"bc-templates","title":"Templates","html":"<p>Copy these for your exercises. They also double as checklists when you evaluate AI-written versions of the same documents.</p><h4 id=\"prd-one-page\">PRD (one page)</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code># [Feature name]\n\nProblem: Who has what pain, how often, and the evidence.\nGoal &amp; success metrics: Outcome + metric (numerator / denominator, target, by when).\nNon-goals: What this will NOT do.\nUsers &amp; key scenarios: Persona(s) and 2–3 main use cases.\nFunctional requirements:\n  1. The system must...\n  2. ...\nNon-functional requirements: Performance, reliability, security, privacy, accessibility, data integrity.\nUser flow: Step-by-step, including errors and edge cases.\nAssumptions / constraints / dependencies:\nRisks &amp; open questions:\nRelease approach: Pilot, phases, rollback plan.</code></pre><h4 id=\"user-story-with-acceptance-criteria\">User story with acceptance criteria</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>As a [user], I want [capability], so that [benefit].\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- Given [context], when [action], then [result].\n- Given [unhappy-path context], when [action], then [result].\n- [Rule with a number, e.g. &quot;within 60 seconds&quot;]\n\nOut of scope: [...]</code></pre><h4 id=\"release-notes\">Release notes</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>## [Version] — [Date]\nHighlight: [Biggest user benefit in one sentence]\n\nNew: [Benefit first] — [what changed]\nImproved: [Benefit first] — [what changed]\nFixed: [What users will notice]\nAction required: [What users must do differently, or &quot;None&quot;]</code></pre><h4 id=\"stakeholder-update-bluf\">Stakeholder update (BLUF)</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>Status: On track / At risk / Off track — [one-line reason]\nSince last update: [Outcomes, not activities]\nMetrics: [Number vs target]\nRisks / blockers: [Risk → impact → mitigation]\nDecision or help needed: [Ask] — [owner] by [date]\nNext: [Top 1–3 items]</code></pre><h4 id=\"decision-document\">Decision document</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>Decision: [What was decided, one sentence]\nContext: [Why a decision was needed]\nOptions considered: A (pros / cons), B (pros / cons), C (pros / cons)\nRationale: [Criteria used and why the chosen option wins]\nTrade-offs accepted: [What we give up]\nConsequences / follow-ups:\nOwner &amp; date:</code></pre><h4 id=\"evaluation-rationale-single-response\">Evaluation rationale (single response)</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>Score: [X/5] — [one-line verdict]\nInstruction following: [Met / partly / missed] — &quot;[quoted evidence]&quot; ([n] of [n] requirements)\nConstraints: [Length, format, required sections] — [counts]\nAccuracy: [Errors or inconsistencies, quoted]\nAudience fit &amp; usefulness: [Who it&#x27;s for; can they act on it?]\nClarity &amp; tone: [Specific observation]\nStrengths: [What works]\nFix: [What would raise the score]</code></pre><h4 id=\"evaluation-rationale-side-by-side\">Evaluation rationale (side by side)</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>Preference: [A / B] is [much / slightly] better.\nDeciding factor: [The criterion that decided it, with quoted evidence from both]\nA: [Key strength] / [key weakness]\nB: [Key strength] / [key weakness]\nWhat would change my preference: [...]</code></pre><h4 id=\"escalation-note\">Escalation note</h4><pre class=\"tpl\"><code>Ambiguity: [What&#x27;s unclear]\nMissing rule: [What the guidelines don&#x27;t cover]\nDecision needed: [The question, in one sentence]\nWhat I did meanwhile: [How I scored it and flagged it]</code></pre>","secs":[{"id":"prd-one-page","t":"PRD (one page)"},{"id":"user-story-with-acceptance-criteria","t":"User story with acceptance criteria"},{"id":"release-notes","t":"Release notes"},{"id":"stakeholder-update-bluf","t":"Stakeholder update (BLUF)"},{"id":"decision-document","t":"Decision document"},{"id":"evaluation-rationale-single-response","t":"Evaluation rationale (single response)"},{"id":"evaluation-rationale-side-by-side","t":"Evaluation rationale (side by side)"},{"id":"escalation-note","t":"Escalation note"}],"words":417}]},{"id":"book","title":"The Flash Protocol: PM/PO Mastery Book","short":"Mastery Book","intro":"<p>The book after the bootcamp. The 7-Day Bootcamp gave you the vocabulary; this book trains the judgment that separates a Senior PM/PO evaluator from a Junior one, through scenarios, drills and a memory system built to make it stick. Read a module, drill it, then let me quiz you until the answers come without thinking.</p>","chapters":[{"id":"bk-how-this-book-works","title":"How this book works","html":"<p>There&#x27;s no instant download for skills, but there&#x27;s a fast way: short bursts of input, immediate practice, and repetition timed so your brain keeps it. Every module follows the same loop.</p><ol><li><strong>Flash</strong>: read one short module (15–20 minutes). Each idea has a hook so it&#x27;s easy to recall.</li><li><strong>Drill</strong>: answer its scenario or rapid-fire questions out loud, then in writing.</li><li><strong>Check</strong>: compare your answer with the Senior model answer. Mark what you missed.</li><li><strong>Review</strong>: revisit on the spaced schedule (Day 1, 2, 4, 7, 14, 30) using the flashcards.</li><li><strong>Teach back</strong>: explain it in your own words, to me or to anyone, in under 2 minutes.</li></ol><p><strong>How we use it together.</strong> In any chat, say one of these and I&#x27;ll run the session:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>You say</th><th>I do</th></tr><tr><td>&quot;Flash me Module A&quot;</td><td>Quick summary, then 5 rapid-fire questions; I grade each answer</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Scenario drill&quot;</td><td>I give you a fresh case; you answer; I score it Junior / Mid / Senior with a rationale</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Grade my rationale&quot;</td><td>You paste an evaluation you wrote; I critique it like a QA reviewer</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Flashcards&quot;</td><td>An interactive card deck in the chat, from this book</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Mock interview&quot;</td><td>I ask PM/PO evaluation questions one at a time and give feedback after each</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Prerequisite.</strong> Finish the 7-Day Product Evaluation Bootcamp first, or at least Days 1–5. This book assumes that vocabulary.</p>","secs":[],"words":208},{"id":"bk-the-level-ladder","title":"The level ladder","html":"<p>Seniority shows in how you reason, not how much you know. A Junior notices problems, a Mid-level explains them against the rules, and a Senior judges which ones matter, why, and what to do, with evidence.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Junior</th><th>Mid-level</th><th>Senior</th></tr><tr><td>Spotting issues</td><td>Notices something feels off</td><td>Names the issue and the rule it breaks</td><td>Ranks issues by severity and impact on the reader</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Paraphrases</td><td>Quotes the problem text</td><td>Quotes, counts and shows the consequence</td></tr><tr><td>Criteria</td><td>Uses personal taste</td><td>Applies the rubric correctly</td><td>Applies the rubric and explains edge cases the rubric doesn&#x27;t cover</td></tr><tr><td>Audience</td><td>&quot;It&#x27;s clear&quot;</td><td>&quot;It&#x27;s clear for engineers but too technical for users&quot;</td><td>&quot;Users can&#x27;t act on it: no next step; the exec version buries the decision in paragraph 3&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Trade-offs</td><td>Picks a side</td><td>Lists pros and cons</td><td>Decides, names what&#x27;s given up, and says what would change the decision</td></tr><tr><td>Ambiguity</td><td>Guesses</td><td>Asks a question</td><td>Makes a reasonable call, documents it, flags the guideline gap</td></tr><tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Varies with mood</td><td>Consistent within a session</td><td>Consistent across hundreds of items; recalibrates against gold examples</td></tr><tr><td>Writing</td><td>Long and vague</td><td>Structured</td><td>Short, structured, specific; every sentence earns its place</td></tr><tr><td>Product sense</td><td>Judges the words</td><td>Judges whether the content meets the prompt</td><td>Judges whether it would work for the real business and user</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>The Senior test, in one question:</strong> <em>Could another expert read your rationale and reach the same score without seeing the response?</em> If yes, you&#x27;re writing at Senior level.</p><p><strong>Where you are now (honest read):</strong> strong Junior to Mid-level in evaluation (new to it, one month in), with Senior-level instincts in systems and real-world business judgment from your client work. The fastest gains are in evidence, criteria and written structure, which is exactly what this book drills.</p>","secs":[],"words":248},{"id":"bk-the-flash-protocol-how-you-ll-remember-all-of-th","title":"The Flash Protocol: how you'll remember all of this","html":"<p>Reading feels like learning, but recall is what builds memory. These five techniques are well supported by learning research; use all five.</p><h4 id=\"1-active-recall-test-don-t-reread\">1. Active recall (test, don&#x27;t reread)</h4><p>After each module, close it and write everything you remember. Then check. The struggle to retrieve is what strengthens memory. Rereading feels productive but sticks far less.</p><h4 id=\"2-spaced-repetition-beat-the-forgetting-curve\">2. Spaced repetition (beat the forgetting curve)</h4><p>Memory fades fast after first learning (the <strong>Ebbinghaus forgetting curve</strong>), and each well-timed review slows the fade. Review each module on this schedule:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Review</th><th>When</th><th>Takes</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Same day, before sleep</td><td>5 min</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Next day</td><td>5 min</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Day 4</td><td>5 min</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Day 7</td><td>10 min</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Day 14</td><td>10 min</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Day 30</td><td>10 min</td></tr></table></div><p>Use the flashcard deck at the end, or the <strong>Leitner box</strong>: cards you get right move to a less frequent pile; cards you miss go back to daily.</p><h4 id=\"3-memory-palace-your-career-as-the-building\">3. Memory palace (your career as the building)</h4><p>The <strong>method of loci</strong> attaches ideas to places you know well. Your career is already a building you can walk through. Walk it in order and &quot;see&quot; each concept in its room:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Room (from your life)</th><th>What lives there</th><th>Picture it</th></tr><tr><td><strong>TESDA / UPH classroom</strong></td><td>Foundations: problem before solution, outcomes vs outputs</td><td>A whiteboard: &quot;PROBLEM&quot; written big, &quot;solution&quot; small underneath</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Netmarks office, GSK DMS project</strong></td><td>Specs: PRD sections, acceptance criteria, Definition of Done</td><td>You at a UAT table, ticking a printed checklist: Given / When / Then</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TeleTech data center</strong></td><td>Non-functional requirements: performance, reliability, security</td><td>Server racks with labels: SPEED, UPTIME, LOCK, SCALE</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DBM training room (Incuventure)</strong></td><td>Audience writing: exec vs technical vs end user</td><td>Three chairs: a director, an engineer, a clerk, each holding a different version of your slides</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Your Chatbot Authority desk</strong></td><td>Product thinking: JTBD, metrics, prioritization, trade-offs</td><td>A client lead report: 6 hrs → 7 min circled in red</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Your micro1 workspace</strong></td><td>Evaluation: rubric, verdict → rule → evidence → impact → fix</td><td>A scorecard with five boxes filled left to right</td></tr></table></div><p>Before an interview, walk the building in your mind once. Each room hands you its frameworks.</p><h4 id=\"4-elaboration-connect-to-your-own-work\">4. Elaboration (connect to your own work)</h4><p>For every new term, write one sentence: <em>&quot;I did this when…&quot;</em> If you can&#x27;t, write <em>&quot;I&#x27;d use this when…&quot;</em> Connected knowledge is recalled far more easily than isolated facts.</p><h4 id=\"5-teach-back-the-feynman-technique\">5. Teach-back (the Feynman technique)</h4><ol><li>Pick a concept. 2. Explain it as if to a 12-year-old. 3. Where you get stuck or use jargon, that&#x27;s your gap: go back and relearn it. 4. Simplify again. If you can teach it simply, you own it.</li></ol><h4 id=\"daily-dose-45-60-minutes\">Daily dose (45–60 minutes)</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Activity</th></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Flashcards due today (spaced review)</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Flash one new section of a module</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>One Scenario Lab case, written</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Five rapid-fire drills, out loud</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Teach back one concept, out loud</td></tr></table></div>","secs":[{"id":"1-active-recall-test-don-t-reread","t":"1. Active recall (test, don't reread)"},{"id":"2-spaced-repetition-beat-the-forgetting-curve","t":"2. Spaced repetition (beat the forgetting curve)"},{"id":"3-memory-palace-your-career-as-the-building","t":"3. Memory palace (your career as the building)"},{"id":"4-elaboration-connect-to-your-own-work","t":"4. Elaboration (connect to your own work)"},{"id":"5-teach-back-the-feynman-technique","t":"5. Teach-back (the Feynman technique)"},{"id":"daily-dose-45-60-minutes","t":"Daily dose (45–60 minutes)"}],"words":416},{"id":"bk-mnemonic-master-list","title":"Mnemonic master list","html":"<p>One hook per framework. Say each hook out loud until it comes back without effort; the hook pulls the whole framework with it.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Framework</th><th>Hook to memorize</th><th>What it unpacks to</th></tr><tr><td>Rationale shape</td><td><strong>&quot;Very Rarely Experts Ignore Facts&quot;</strong> (VREIF)</td><td>Verdict → Rule → Evidence → Impact → Fix</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluation order</td><td><strong>&quot;I Can Always Use Clear, Tight Copy&quot;</strong></td><td>Instructions → Constraints → Accuracy → Usefulness &amp; audience → Clarity → Tone → Concision</td></tr><tr><td>Failure modes</td><td><strong>&quot;3F · 3C · 3T&quot;</strong></td><td><strong>F</strong>luent-but-wrong, <strong>F</strong>iller, <strong>F</strong>abrication (hallucination) · <strong>C</strong>onstraint breach, <strong>C</strong>ontradiction, s<strong>C</strong>ope creep · wrong <strong>T</strong>one, wrong <strong>T</strong>arget audience, <strong>T</strong>ruckling (sycophancy)</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td><strong>&quot;Trust, Task, Time, Taste&quot;</strong></td><td>Critical breaks <em>trust</em> (false, unsafe) · Major breaks the <em>task</em> (missed requirement) · Minor costs the reader <em>time</em> (unclear) · Nit is <em>taste</em> (style)</td></tr><tr><td>Audiences</td><td><strong>&quot;Execs: WHY + ASK. Engineers: HOW + EDGE. Users: WHAT + NEXT.&quot;</strong></td><td>What each audience needs first</td></tr><tr><td>PRD sections</td><td><strong>&quot;Pretty Good Notes Usually Require Fixing All Risky Releases&quot;</strong></td><td>Problem, Goals, Non-goals, Users, Requirements, Flows, Assumptions/constraints, Risks, Release</td></tr><tr><td>Non-functional requirements</td><td><strong>&quot;PRISM-SAD&quot;</strong></td><td>Performance, Reliability, Integrity (data), Security, Maintainability · Scalability, Accessibility, Data privacy</td></tr><tr><td>Good user story</td><td><strong>INVEST</strong></td><td>Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable</td></tr><tr><td>Acceptance criteria</td><td><strong>&quot;Scene, Act, Proof&quot;</strong></td><td>Given (scene) → When (act) → Then (proof)</td></tr><tr><td>Product risks (Marty Cagan)</td><td><strong>&quot;Want it? Use it? Build it? Should we?&quot;</strong></td><td>Value, Usability, Feasibility, Business viability</td></tr><tr><td>Prioritization</td><td><strong>RICE</strong> = &quot;Reach Into Customers&#x27; Experience&quot;</td><td>(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td><strong>MoSCoW</strong></td><td>Must, Should, Could, Won&#x27;t (this time)</td></tr><tr><td>Stakeholder update</td><td><strong>&quot;Smart Captains Measure Risks And Navigate&quot;</strong></td><td>Status, Changes, Metrics, Risks, Ask, Next</td></tr><tr><td>Release notes</td><td><strong>&quot;Happy News Is Fixed Always&quot;</strong></td><td>Highlight, New, Improved, Fixed, Action required</td></tr><tr><td>Decision doc</td><td><strong>&quot;Don&#x27;t Choose Options Randomly; Trade-offs Count&quot;</strong></td><td>Decision, Context, Options, Rationale, Trade-offs, Consequences</td></tr><tr><td>Exec writing</td><td><strong>BLUF</strong></td><td>Bottom line up front</td></tr><tr><td>Proposal logic</td><td><strong>SCQA</strong> = &quot;Some Clients Question Answers&quot;</td><td>Situation, Complication, Question, Answer</td></tr><tr><td>Metrics</td><td><strong>&quot;Count it, cap it, clock it&quot;</strong></td><td>Numerator ÷ denominator, over a time window</td></tr><tr><td>Interview stories</td><td><strong>STAR 2-1-6-1</strong></td><td>Situation 20%, Task 10%, Action 60%, Result 10%</td></tr><tr><td>Ambiguity note</td><td><strong>&quot;Gap, Rule, Ask, Act&quot;</strong></td><td>The ambiguity, the missing rule, the decision needed, what you did meanwhile</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Drill:</strong> cover the right two columns. Say each hook, then unpack it. Any you can&#x27;t fully unpack go on today&#x27;s flashcard pile.</p>","secs":[],"words":304},{"id":"bk-module-a-senior-product-thinking","title":"Module A — Senior product thinking","html":"<p>Senior product people judge every document by one question: <em>does this lead to building the right thing, for the right reason, with risks understood?</em> Learn these lenses and you&#x27;ll see weaknesses in specs that others miss.</p><h4 id=\"a1-the-four-product-risks-marty-cagan-inspired\">A1. The four product risks (Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em>)</h4><p>Every product idea can fail in four ways. A strong spec addresses all four.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Question</th><th>What a spec should show</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Value</strong></td><td>Will customers want it?</td><td>Evidence of the problem; who asked; data</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Usability</strong></td><td>Can users figure it out?</td><td>User flow, prototype or testing plan</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Feasibility</strong></td><td>Can we build it with our time, skills and tech?</td><td>Engineering input, constraints, dependencies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Viability</strong></td><td>Does it work for the business? (cost, legal, brand, sales)</td><td>Cost, compliance, support impact</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Evaluator move:</strong> when a spec only describes features, note which risks it ignores. &quot;The spec defines the feature but gives no evidence of user demand (value risk) and no cost estimate for SMS volume (viability risk).&quot;</p><h4 id=\"a2-discovery-done-well\">A2. Discovery done well</h4><ul><li><strong>Problem interviews</strong>: ask about past behavior (&quot;Tell me about the last time…&quot;), not opinions (&quot;Would you use…?&quot;).</li><li><strong>Opportunity Solution Tree</strong> (Teresa Torres): Outcome → Opportunities (user needs and pains) → Solutions → Experiments. It keeps solutions tied to an outcome.</li><li><strong>Assumption testing</strong>: identify the riskiest assumption and test it cheaply first (a landing page, a manual pilot, a prototype).</li><li><strong>Your example:</strong> a pilot go-live with a small group before full rollout is assumption testing at the rollout level.</li></ul><h4 id=\"a3-metrics-that-mean-something\">A3. Metrics that mean something</h4><ul><li><strong>Metric tree</strong>: break the North Star into drivers. <em>Booked appointments = leads × response rate × qualification rate × booking rate.</em> Each driver is something a team can move.</li><li><strong>Counter-metrics</strong> (guardrails): protect against gaming. If you push response speed, watch answer accuracy and opt-out rate.</li><li><strong>AARRR</strong> (Dave McClure&#x27;s &quot;pirate metrics&quot;): Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Referral, Revenue.</li><li><strong>HEART</strong> (Google): Happiness, Engagement, Adoption, Retention, Task success. Good for user-experience quality.</li><li><strong>Vanity metric</strong>: looks good but doesn&#x27;t drive decisions (total chats, followers). Pick Up Trip taught you this: chats rose, but transactions are what counted.</li></ul><h4 id=\"a4-trade-offs-and-saying-no\">A4. Trade-offs and saying no</h4><ul><li>Every &quot;yes&quot; is a &quot;no&quot; to something else (<strong>opportunity cost</strong>).</li><li>Senior trade-off statement: <em>&quot;We&#x27;ll choose X over Y because [criterion]. We accept [cost]. We&#x27;d revisit if [signal].&quot;</em></li><li><strong>Reversible vs irreversible decisions</strong> (&quot;two-way vs one-way doors&quot;): move fast on reversible ones, slow down on irreversible ones.</li></ul><h4 id=\"a5-strategy-in-one-page\">A5. Strategy in one page</h4><ul><li><strong>Vision</strong>: the future you&#x27;re building toward. <strong>Strategy</strong>: the choices about where to play and how to win. <strong>Roadmap</strong>: the sequence of bets.</li><li>Good strategy has a <strong>diagnosis</strong> (what&#x27;s really going on), a <strong>guiding policy</strong> (the approach), and <strong>coherent actions</strong> (Richard Rumelt, <em>Good Strategy / Bad Strategy</em>).</li><li>Bad strategy is a list of goals with no diagnosis.</li></ul><h4 id=\"module-a-drills\">Module A drills</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.11971-0\"><span>Take one of your client projects. Write the four risks and how each was (or wasn&#x27;t) handled.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.11971-1\"><span>Build a metric tree for a clinic&#x27;s booking funnel, with one counter-metric.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.11971-2\"><span>Write a trade-off statement for choosing speed over accuracy in a chatbot&#x27;s first version.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.11971-3\"><span>Spot the vanity metric in this claim: &quot;Our bot handled 40,000 messages last month.&quot; What would you ask for instead?</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"a1-the-four-product-risks-marty-cagan-inspired","t":"A1. The four product risks (Marty Cagan, Inspired)"},{"id":"a2-discovery-done-well","t":"A2. Discovery done well"},{"id":"a3-metrics-that-mean-something","t":"A3. Metrics that mean something"},{"id":"a4-trade-offs-and-saying-no","t":"A4. Trade-offs and saying no"},{"id":"a5-strategy-in-one-page","t":"A5. Strategy in one page"},{"id":"module-a-drills","t":"Module A drills"}],"words":482},{"id":"bk-module-b-senior-spec-craft","title":"Module B — Senior spec craft","html":"<p>A Junior reads a spec and asks &quot;is it complete?&quot; A Senior stress-tests it: &quot;where would two engineers build different things from this, and where would a user get stuck?&quot;</p><h4 id=\"b1-the-10-point-spec-stress-test\">B1. The 10-point spec stress test</h4><p>Run every specification through these questions. Each &quot;no&quot; is a finding.</p><ol><li><strong>Problem first?</strong> Is the user problem stated before the solution, with evidence?</li><li><strong>Measurable success?</strong> Is there a metric with a number and a time frame?</li><li><strong>Scope edges?</strong> Are non-goals stated?</li><li><strong>Testable requirements?</strong> Could QA write pass/fail tests from each requirement?</li><li><strong>Unhappy paths?</strong> Are errors, empty states, timeouts and invalid input covered?</li><li><strong>Non-functional needs?</strong> Speed, reliability, security, privacy, accessibility stated where they matter?</li><li><strong>No hidden solutions?</strong> Are requirements about <em>what</em>, not prematurely <em>how</em>?</li><li><strong>Unambiguous words?</strong> No undefined &quot;fast&quot;, &quot;simple&quot;, &quot;intuitive&quot;, &quot;seamless&quot;, &quot;robust&quot;?</li><li><strong>Consistent?</strong> Do numbers, names and dates match across sections?</li><li><strong>Right for its reader?</strong> Does it serve the engineers and testers who&#x27;ll use it?</li></ol><h4 id=\"b2-ambiguity-hunting\">B2. Ambiguity hunting</h4><p>The most valuable thing you can do to a spec is find the sentence two people would read differently.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Ambiguous</th><th>Why</th><th>Precise version</th></tr><tr><td>&quot;Notify the user quickly.&quot;</td><td>How quick? Which channel?</td><td>&quot;Send an SMS within 60 seconds.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Support large files.&quot;</td><td>How large?</td><td>&quot;Accept files up to 25 MB; reject larger with a clear error.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Only admins and managers can edit.&quot;</td><td>Admins <em>and</em> managers, or only users with <em>both</em> roles?</td><td>&quot;Users with either the Admin or Manager role can edit.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Remove duplicates.&quot;</td><td>Duplicate by what? Which record survives?</td><td>&quot;Match on normalized phone OR lowercased email; keep the oldest record; log the merge.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;The system should handle errors gracefully.&quot;</td><td>Means nothing testable</td><td>&quot;On payment failure, show the reason, keep the cart, and offer retry.&quot;</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Words that should trigger your attention:</strong> quickly, easily, simple, intuitive, user-friendly, robust, seamless, appropriate, etc., and/or, some, many, usually, should (vs must).</p><h4 id=\"b3-acceptance-criteria-quality\">B3. Acceptance criteria quality</h4><p>Senior-quality criteria are:</p><ul><li><strong>Binary</strong>: pass or fail, no judgment calls.</li><li><strong>Observable</strong>: describe outcomes a tester can see, not internal code.</li><li><strong>Complete</strong>: happy path + at least the main unhappy paths.</li><li><strong>Independent of each other</strong>: each criterion tests one thing.</li><li><strong>Free of solutions</strong>: &quot;user can reset password via email link&quot;, not &quot;use library X&quot;.</li></ul><h4 id=\"b4-spotting-what-s-missing\">B4. Spotting what&#x27;s missing</h4><p>Fluent specs often omit the hard parts. Check specifically for:</p><ul><li><strong>Data</strong>: where it comes from, who owns it, how long it&#x27;s kept, what happens on deletion.</li><li><strong>Permissions</strong>: who can see and do what.</li><li><strong>States</strong>: every status an item can be in, and what moves it between them.</li><li><strong>Migration</strong>: what happens to existing users and data on launch.</li><li><strong>Rollback</strong>: how to undo it if it goes wrong.</li><li><strong>Support impact</strong>: what customer support needs to know.</li></ul><p>Your CRM audits (duplicates, broken triggers, leaking stages) are exactly this skill: finding the states and data rules nobody specified.</p><h4 id=\"module-b-drills\">Module B drills</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.15410-0\"><span>Ask an AI assistant to write a PRD for &quot;missed-call text-back&quot;. Run the 10-point stress test; write each finding in VREIF format.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.15410-1\"><span>Rewrite 5 ambiguous requirements from any real tool&#x27;s help docs into precise ones.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.15410-2\"><span>For a lead pipeline, list every state a lead can be in and what moves it to the next one.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.15410-3\"><span>Write the &quot;what&#x27;s missing&quot; list for a spec that only has a feature description.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"b1-the-10-point-spec-stress-test","t":"B1. The 10-point spec stress test"},{"id":"b2-ambiguity-hunting","t":"B2. Ambiguity hunting"},{"id":"b3-acceptance-criteria-quality","t":"B3. Acceptance criteria quality"},{"id":"b4-spotting-what-s-missing","t":"B4. Spotting what's missing"},{"id":"module-b-drills","t":"Module B drills"}],"words":487},{"id":"bk-module-c-senior-writing-judgment","title":"Module C — Senior writing judgment","html":"<p>Good product writing is judged by what the reader can do after reading it. A Senior evaluator asks three questions in order: <em>Who is reading? What must they know or do? Does the text get them there with the least effort?</em></p><h4 id=\"c1-the-audience-matrix\">C1. The audience matrix</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th></th><th>Executive</th><th>Technical</th><th>End user</th></tr><tr><td><strong>First line should be</strong></td><td>The decision or status</td><td>The precise change or requirement</td><td>The benefit to them</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Length</strong></td><td>Shortest</td><td>As long as precision needs</td><td>Short</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Numbers</strong></td><td>Business impact (revenue, risk, time)</td><td>Exact values, limits, versions</td><td>Only if they matter to the user</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Jargon</strong></td><td>None</td><td>Expected</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Must include</strong></td><td>The ask, owner, date</td><td>Edge cases, dependencies</td><td>What to do next</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Red flag</strong></td><td>Detail before the point</td><td>Vague words</td><td>Internal ticket IDs, blame</td></tr></table></div><h4 id=\"c2-tone-calibration\">C2. Tone calibration</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Right tone</th><th>Wrong tone example</th></tr><tr><td>Outage or bad news</td><td>Calm, accountable, specific</td><td>&quot;Oops, small hiccup!&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>New feature</td><td>Clear, confident, benefit-led</td><td>&quot;Revolutionary game-changing AI magic&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Error message</td><td>Neutral, helpful, no blame</td><td>&quot;You entered an invalid value&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Exec update</td><td>Direct, factual, decision-oriented</td><td>Long narrative before the status</td></tr><tr><td>Deprecation or price change</td><td>Honest, early, with help to transition</td><td>Burying the change in paragraph four</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Hedging check:</strong> too much hedging (&quot;might possibly&quot;, &quot;could potentially&quot;) weakens trust; too little overclaims. Match certainty to evidence.</p><h4 id=\"c3-structure-signals\">C3. Structure signals</h4><ul><li><strong>Headline test</strong>: could a reader skimming only the headings and first sentences get the message?</li><li><strong>One idea per paragraph</strong>, topic sentence first.</li><li><strong>Parallel lists</strong>: every bullet in the same grammatical form.</li><li><strong>Front-loading</strong>: the important words go at the start of sentences and bullets.</li><li><strong>Scannable numbers</strong>: &quot;3 of 4&quot;, &quot;60 seconds&quot;, not &quot;a few&quot; or &quot;shortly&quot;.</li></ul><h4 id=\"c4-the-four-writing-failures-to-name-precisely\">C4. The four writing failures to name precisely</h4><ol><li><strong>Buried lede</strong>: the main point appears late. <em>Name it:</em> &quot;The decision appears in paragraph 3; an executive skimming would miss it.&quot;</li><li><strong>Audience mismatch</strong>: right content, wrong reader. <em>Name it:</em> &quot;Uses &#x27;idempotent retries&#x27; in user-facing notes.&quot;</li><li><strong>Empty fluency</strong>: polished sentences with no specifics. <em>Name it:</em> &quot;Three sentences describe &#x27;enhanced experience&#x27; without saying what changed.&quot;</li><li><strong>Missing call to action</strong>: the reader doesn&#x27;t know what to do. <em>Name it:</em> &quot;Doesn&#x27;t tell users they must re-connect their calendar.&quot;</li></ol><h4 id=\"module-c-drills\">Module C drills</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.18146-0\"><span>Rewrite one paragraph from an old client report three times: exec, technical, end user.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.18146-1\"><span>Take 3 real release notes from apps you use. Score each on audience fit and call to action, using VREIF.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.18146-2\"><span>Write a 5-line outage notice for a chatbot that sent wrong prices for 2 hours.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.18146-3\"><span>Find a buried lede in any email you&#x27;ve received this week and rewrite it BLUF.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"c1-the-audience-matrix","t":"C1. The audience matrix"},{"id":"c2-tone-calibration","t":"C2. Tone calibration"},{"id":"c3-structure-signals","t":"C3. Structure signals"},{"id":"c4-the-four-writing-failures-to-name-precisely","t":"C4. The four writing failures to name precisely"},{"id":"module-c-drills","t":"Module C drills"}],"words":357},{"id":"bk-module-d-senior-evaluation","title":"Module D — Senior evaluation","html":"<p>Senior evaluators are trusted because their scores are predictable, defensible and useful to the people improving the model. Three skills get you there: weighing severity, staying calibrated, and handling what the guidelines don&#x27;t cover.</p><h4 id=\"d1-severity-drives-the-score\">D1. Severity drives the score</h4><p>Not all issues are equal. Classify each finding, then let the most severe one anchor your score (&quot;Trust, Task, Time, Taste&quot;).</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Severity</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th><th>Typical effect on a 1–5 score</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Critical</strong> (trust)</td><td>False, unsafe, or misleading content a reader would act on</td><td>Wrong refund policy; invented feature</td><td>Caps the score at 1–2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Major</strong> (task)</td><td>A prompt requirement missed or a constraint broken</td><td>No &quot;action required&quot; line; 40% over the word limit</td><td>Usually caps at 2–3</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Minor</strong> (time)</td><td>Unclear, wordy or awkward, but the task is done</td><td>Buried lede; repetitive sentence</td><td>Drops about one point</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nit</strong> (taste)</td><td>Style preference</td><td>Prefers &quot;use&quot; over &quot;utilize&quot;</td><td>Mention it, but don&#x27;t penalize</td></tr></table></div><p>These caps are a common pattern, not a universal rule. Always follow your project&#x27;s rubric where it differs.</p><h4 id=\"d2-calibration-habits\">D2. Calibration habits</h4><ul><li><strong>Start with anchors</strong>: re-read the score definitions at the start of every session.</li><li><strong>Use reference examples</strong>: keep 3–5 past items you&#x27;re confident about (one per score level) and compare new items against them.</li><li><strong>Check your distribution</strong>: if nearly everything gets a 4, you&#x27;ve probably stopped discriminating.</li><li><strong>Watch your biases</strong>: length bias, order bias (favoring whichever response you read first), and fatigue.</li><li><strong>When you disagree with QA feedback</strong>, find the exact guideline line that explains the gap; update your reference examples.</li></ul><h4 id=\"d3-designing-a-rubric-in-case-you-re-asked-to\">D3. Designing a rubric (in case you&#x27;re asked to)</h4><ol><li>Define the <strong>purpose</strong>: what decision will the scores support?</li><li>Choose <strong>3–6 dimensions</strong> that are distinct (don&#x27;t double-count the same flaw).</li><li>Write <strong>anchors</strong> for each level, with an example for each.</li><li>Define <strong>weights</strong> or a <strong>gating rule</strong> (e.g., any Critical issue = fail).</li><li><strong>Pilot</strong> on 10–20 items with 2+ reviewers; measure agreement; fix confusing anchors.</li></ol><h4 id=\"d4-when-the-guidelines-don-t-cover-it\">D4. When the guidelines don&#x27;t cover it</h4><ol><li>Re-read the guidelines once more; look for the closest analogous rule.</li><li>Make the most reasonable call based on the rule&#x27;s <em>intent</em>.</li><li>Write a short note (&quot;Gap, Rule, Ask, Act&quot;) and flag it.</li><li>Apply the same call consistently until you get an answer.</li><li>Add it to your edge-case log.</li></ol><h4 id=\"d5-pairwise-judgments-at-senior-level\">D5. Pairwise judgments at Senior level</h4><ul><li>Judge each response against the prompt <strong>independently first</strong>, then compare.</li><li>Decide on the <strong>deciding factor</strong>: the single most important difference.</li><li>Calibrate strength: &quot;much better&quot; needs a Major or Critical difference; &quot;slightly better&quot; is a Minor one.</li><li>Say what would flip your preference.</li></ul><h4 id=\"module-d-drills\">Module D drills</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.21207-0\"><span>Classify 10 findings from your earlier exercises by severity.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.21207-1\"><span>Write a 4-dimension rubric with anchors for &quot;stakeholder update quality&quot;.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.21207-2\"><span>Write a &quot;Gap, Rule, Ask, Act&quot; note for: &quot;Prompt says &#x27;brief&#x27;; response is 180 words; guidelines don&#x27;t define brief.&quot;</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.21207-3\"><span>Do one pairwise comparison and state the deciding factor and what would flip it.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"d1-severity-drives-the-score","t":"D1. Severity drives the score"},{"id":"d2-calibration-habits","t":"D2. Calibration habits"},{"id":"d3-designing-a-rubric-in-case-you-re-asked-to","t":"D3. Designing a rubric (in case you're asked to)"},{"id":"d4-when-the-guidelines-don-t-cover-it","t":"D4. When the guidelines don't cover it"},{"id":"d5-pairwise-judgments-at-senior-level","t":"D5. Pairwise judgments at Senior level"},{"id":"module-d-drills","t":"Module D drills"}],"words":431},{"id":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator","title":"Module E — AI product literacy for PM/PO evaluators","html":"<p>More and more specs, release notes and stakeholder updates describe AI features. You don&#x27;t need to build them, but you do need to spot when a document about an AI feature is missing what matters. This module takes the evaluation-relevant parts of the AI Product Management curriculum you shared and puts them in plain language.</p><h4 id=\"e1-ai-features-are-probabilistic\">E1. AI features are probabilistic</h4><ul><li>Traditional software is <strong>deterministic</strong>: same input, same output.</li><li>AI features are <strong>probabilistic</strong>: the same input can produce different outputs, and some will be wrong.</li><li><strong>So a good AI spec defines acceptable error</strong>, not just intended behavior: &quot;≥ 90% of answers grounded in the help-center articles, measured weekly on a 200-question test set.&quot;</li><li><strong>Evaluator move:</strong> flag any AI spec or update that promises perfection (&quot;always accurate&quot;, &quot;never wrong&quot;).</li></ul><h4 id=\"e2-measurable-limits-in-ai-specs\">E2. Measurable limits in AI specs</h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain meaning</th><th>What a good spec says</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Latency budget</strong></td><td>How long a response may take</td><td>&quot;First token within 1 second; full answer within 5 seconds&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>p95 / p99</strong></td><td>95% (or 99%) of requests are at least this fast</td><td>&quot;p95 latency under 2 seconds&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SLA</strong></td><td>A promised service level (uptime, response time)</td><td>&quot;99.9% monthly availability&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Throughput</strong></td><td>How many requests the system handles</td><td>&quot;200 requests per second at peak&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost per query</strong></td><td>What each AI answer costs to produce</td><td>&quot;Under $0.01 per conversation on average&quot;</td></tr></table></div><h4 id=\"e3-grounding-and-rag\">E3. Grounding and RAG</h4><ul><li><strong>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)</strong>: the system first retrieves relevant documents, then the model answers using them.</li><li><strong>Grounded</strong> answer: every claim is supported by the retrieved sources.</li><li><strong>Common failures</strong>: wrong document retrieved; right document, wrong answer; answer adds facts not in the sources (hallucination).</li><li><strong>Evaluator move:</strong> check that claims in an AI-generated summary actually appear in the source, not just that they&#x27;re on the same topic.</li></ul><h4 id=\"e4-guardrails-safety-and-failure-handling\">E4. Guardrails, safety and failure handling</h4><ul><li><strong>Guardrails</strong>: rules and checks on inputs and outputs (blocked topics, required formats, PII removal).</li><li><strong>Prompt injection</strong>: a user or document tricks the AI into ignoring its instructions. The <strong>OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</strong> lists it among the main security risks.</li><li><strong>Fallback / graceful degradation</strong>: what happens when the AI fails or is slow (a default answer, a human handoff, a clear error).</li><li><strong>Human-in-the-loop</strong>: a person reviews or approves before action. You&#x27;ve designed this in your lead systems.</li><li><strong>Evaluator move:</strong> an AI feature spec without fallback behavior or human escalation has a Major gap.</li></ul><h4 id=\"e5-quality-over-time\">E5. Quality over time</h4><ul><li><strong>Evaluation set</strong>: a fixed set of test prompts used to measure the feature before and after changes.</li><li><strong>Data drift / concept drift</strong>: the real-world inputs (or what counts as correct) change over time, so quality quietly drops.</li><li><strong>Regression</strong>: a new version gets worse at something the old one did well.</li><li><strong>Red-teaming</strong>: deliberately trying to break the feature.</li><li><strong>Incident post-mortem</strong>: after a failure, a blameless write-up of what happened, why, impact, and fixes.</li></ul><h4 id=\"e6-business-decisions-about-ai\">E6. Business decisions about AI</h4><ul><li><strong>Build vs buy vs fine-tune</strong>: use an existing AI API (fast, variable cost), adapt a model to your data (more control, more effort), or build in-house (most control, most cost).</li><li><strong>Unit economics</strong>: cost per user or per query vs revenue per user. AI features can lose money at scale if costs aren&#x27;t planned.</li><li><strong>Communicating uncertainty to executives</strong>: give ranges and confidence, not promises. &quot;We expect 85–90% accuracy at launch, improving as we add examples; the main risk is X.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"e7-the-ai-feature-spec-checklist\">E7. The AI feature spec checklist</h4><p>Add these to your 10-point stress test when a spec describes an AI feature:</p><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-0\"><span>Acceptable error rate and how it&#x27;s measured</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-1\"><span>Evaluation set and quality metrics</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-2\"><span>Latency and cost limits</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-3\"><span>Grounding sources (if it answers from documents)</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-4\"><span>Guardrails: what it must never do</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-5\"><span>Fallback and human escalation</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-6\"><span>Monitoring for drift after launch</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25168-7\"><span>How users are told it&#x27;s AI and what to do when it&#x27;s wrong</span></label></li></ul><h4 id=\"module-e-drills\">Module E drills</h4><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25501-0\"><span>Ask an AI assistant for a one-page spec of &quot;AI reply suggestions for a clinic&#x27;s Messenger inbox&quot;. Run the AI feature checklist and list every gap.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25501-1\"><span>Write an exec update for an AI chatbot launch that communicates uncertainty honestly.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.25501-2\"><span>Explain p95 latency and RAG to a non-technical client in 3 sentences each.</span></label></li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"e1-ai-features-are-probabilistic","t":"E1. AI features are probabilistic"},{"id":"e2-measurable-limits-in-ai-specs","t":"E2. Measurable limits in AI specs"},{"id":"e3-grounding-and-rag","t":"E3. Grounding and RAG"},{"id":"e4-guardrails-safety-and-failure-handling","t":"E4. Guardrails, safety and failure handling"},{"id":"e5-quality-over-time","t":"E5. Quality over time"},{"id":"e6-business-decisions-about-ai","t":"E6. Business decisions about AI"},{"id":"e7-the-ai-feature-spec-checklist","t":"E7. The AI feature spec checklist"},{"id":"module-e-drills","t":"Module E drills"}],"words":629},{"id":"bk-scenario-lab","title":"Scenario Lab","html":"<p>Write your own answer before reading the model answers. Then mark which level yours matched, and what the Senior answer had that yours didn&#x27;t.</p><h4 id=\"scenario-1-the-fluent-release-note\">Scenario 1 — The fluent release note</h4><p><strong>Prompt given to the AI:</strong> &quot;Release notes for clinic receptionists. Under 80 words. Must say what they need to do differently.&quot; <strong>AI response:</strong> &quot;We&#x27;re thrilled to unveil our reimagined scheduling experience! Powered by intelligent automation, appointment management is now smoother, faster and more delightful than ever. Enjoy the future of front-desk efficiency!&quot;</p><ul><li><strong>Junior:</strong> &quot;Too much marketing language. 2/5.&quot;</li><li><strong>Mid:</strong> &quot;Fails the requirement to say what users must do differently; tone is promotional. 2/5.&quot;</li><li><strong>Senior:</strong> &quot;<strong>2/5.</strong> <strong>Instruction following (Major):</strong> the prompt requires what receptionists must do differently; the response never says what changed or what to do. <strong>Empty fluency:</strong> &#x27;smoother, faster and more delightful&#x27; describes no specific change. <strong>Audience:</strong> receptionists need the practical change, not &#x27;the future of front-desk efficiency&#x27;. <strong>Constraint:</strong> 29 words, within limit. <strong>Fix:</strong> name the actual change and the one action needed.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"scenario-2-the-over-long-exec-update\">Scenario 2 — The over-long exec update</h4><p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Exec update on the CRM migration. 5 bullets max. Lead with status.&quot; <strong>Response:</strong> 7 bullets; status (&quot;at risk&quot;) appears in bullet 6; detailed field-mapping notes in bullets 1–4.</p><ul><li><strong>Senior rationale:</strong> &quot;<strong>2/5.</strong> <strong>Constraint (Major):</strong> 7 bullets against a 5-bullet limit. <strong>Instruction (Major):</strong> status must lead; it&#x27;s in bullet 6, so an executive skimming would miss that the migration is at risk. <strong>Audience:</strong> bullets 1–4 are field-mapping detail an executive can&#x27;t act on. <strong>Strength:</strong> the risk itself is clearly explained in bullet 6. <strong>Fix:</strong> lead with &#x27;At risk: launch may slip 1 week due to X; decision needed on Y by Friday&#x27;, then 4 short bullets.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"scenario-3-pairwise-two-acceptance-criteria-sets\">Scenario 3 — Pairwise: two acceptance criteria sets</h4><p><strong>Story:</strong> &quot;As a sales rep, I want leads auto-assigned so I can respond fast.&quot; <strong>A:</strong> &quot;Leads are assigned quickly and fairly to reps.&quot; <strong>B:</strong> &quot;Given a new lead, when it&#x27;s created during business hours, then it&#x27;s assigned round-robin to an active rep within 30 seconds. Given no rep is active, then it&#x27;s assigned to the sales manager and flagged.&quot;</p><ul><li><strong>Senior:</strong> &quot;<strong>B is much better.</strong> Deciding factor: <strong>testability.</strong> A uses &#x27;quickly&#x27; and &#x27;fairly&#x27;, neither of which can be tested. B defines timing (30 seconds), method (round-robin), condition (business hours) and an unhappy path (no active rep). <strong>B&#x27;s gap:</strong> it doesn&#x27;t cover leads created outside business hours. <strong>What would flip it:</strong> nothing. A can&#x27;t be tested as written.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"scenario-4-the-hallucinated-feature\">Scenario 4 — The hallucinated feature</h4><p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Summarize this changelog for customers.&quot; The changelog lists: CSV export added, search bug fixed. <strong>Response:</strong> &quot;New: CSV and PDF export. Fixed: search bug. Improved: dashboard loads 2× faster.&quot;</p><ul><li><strong>Senior:</strong> &quot;<strong>1/5.</strong> <strong>Accuracy (Critical):</strong> &#x27;PDF export&#x27; and &#x27;dashboard loads 2× faster&#x27; are not in the source changelog. Customers would look for features that don&#x27;t exist, which damages trust. The correct items (CSV export, search fix) are present. <strong>Fix:</strong> remove anything not in the source.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"scenario-5-complete-but-unusable\">Scenario 5 — Complete but unusable</h4><p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Explain the new duplicate-merge feature to small-business owners.&quot; <strong>Response:</strong> a correct, complete explanation of normalized E.164 matching, merge precedence and audit logging.</p><ul><li><strong>Senior:</strong> &quot;<strong>3/5.</strong> <strong>Accuracy and completeness:</strong> strong; the logic is correct. <strong>Audience fit (Major):</strong> small-business owners don&#x27;t need E.164 or merge precedence; they need &#x27;you&#x27;ll stop seeing the same customer twice, and nothing is lost&#x27;. <strong>Usefulness:</strong> no next step. <strong>Fix:</strong> lead with the benefit, one sentence on how it works, and where to review merged contacts.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"scenario-6-ambiguous-guideline\">Scenario 6 — Ambiguous guideline</h4><p><strong>Situation:</strong> The rubric says penalize &quot;unprofessional tone&quot;. The response, for a youth sports club newsletter, is casual and uses one emoji.</p><ul><li><strong>Senior:</strong> Scores tone against the <strong>intended audience</strong> (casual is appropriate for a club newsletter), notes the judgment explicitly, and writes a &quot;Gap, Rule, Ask, Act&quot; note: &quot;Gap: &#x27;unprofessional&#x27; isn&#x27;t defined relative to audience. Rule: none for informal audiences. Ask: should tone be judged against the audience in the prompt? Act: judged it appropriate for the stated audience; flagged.&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"scenario-7-the-ai-feature-spec\">Scenario 7 — The AI feature spec</h4><p><strong>Spec excerpt:</strong> &quot;The AI assistant will always answer patient questions accurately using our clinic information.&quot;</p><ul><li><strong>Senior findings:</strong> &quot;&#x27;Always accurate&#x27; is not achievable or testable (define an accuracy target and how it&#x27;s measured). No grounding source or update process for &#x27;clinic information&#x27;. No fallback or human handoff for medical questions (Critical, given patient safety). No guardrail stating what it must never do (diagnose, give dosage advice).&quot;</li></ul><h4 id=\"scenario-8-interview-curveball\">Scenario 8 — Interview curveball</h4><p><strong>Question:</strong> &quot;A response is beautifully written and accurate, but 30% over the word limit. Another is plain, within the limit, and meets every requirement. Which is better?&quot;</p><ul><li><strong>Senior answer:</strong> &quot;The second, unless the guidelines say otherwise. The word limit is an explicit constraint, so exceeding it by 30% is a Major issue, while plain style is at most a Minor one. I&#x27;d note the first response&#x27;s strengths in the rationale, because they show the model can write well, but the deciding factor is instruction compliance. If the limit were soft, I&#x27;d check the guidelines before scoring.&quot;</li></ul>","secs":[{"id":"scenario-1-the-fluent-release-note","t":"Scenario 1 — The fluent release note"},{"id":"scenario-2-the-over-long-exec-update","t":"Scenario 2 — The over-long exec update"},{"id":"scenario-3-pairwise-two-acceptance-criteria-sets","t":"Scenario 3 — Pairwise: two acceptance criteria sets"},{"id":"scenario-4-the-hallucinated-feature","t":"Scenario 4 — The hallucinated feature"},{"id":"scenario-5-complete-but-unusable","t":"Scenario 5 — Complete but unusable"},{"id":"scenario-6-ambiguous-guideline","t":"Scenario 6 — Ambiguous guideline"},{"id":"scenario-7-the-ai-feature-spec","t":"Scenario 7 — The AI feature spec"},{"id":"scenario-8-interview-curveball","t":"Scenario 8 — Interview curveball"}],"words":819},{"id":"bk-rapid-fire-drills","title":"Rapid-fire drills","html":"<p>Answer each out loud in under 60 seconds. Pick 5 per day at random. If you hesitate or ramble, mark it and repeat it tomorrow.</p><p><strong>Foundations</strong></p><ol><li>Product manager vs project manager, in two sentences.</li><li>Output vs outcome, with an example from your work.</li><li>What are the four product risks?</li><li>What&#x27;s a counter-metric, and why does it matter?</li><li>When would you use MoSCoW instead of RICE?</li></ol><p><strong>Specs</strong></p><ol><li>What makes an acceptance criterion testable?</li><li>Name five words that signal ambiguity in a spec.</li><li>Functional vs non-functional requirement, with one example each.</li><li>What belongs in a &quot;non-goals&quot; section, and why include it?</li><li>What&#x27;s missing from a spec that only describes the happy path?</li></ol><p><strong>Writing</strong></p><ol><li>What does an executive need in the first line?</li><li>What&#x27;s a buried lede? How do you name it in a rationale?</li><li>How should an error message be written?</li><li>Changelog vs release notes?</li><li>What&#x27;s &quot;empty fluency&quot;?</li></ol><p><strong>Evaluation</strong></p><ol><li>Recite VREIF and explain each step.</li><li>Recite the evaluation order (&quot;I Can Always Use Clear, Tight Copy&quot;).</li><li>Critical vs Major vs Minor vs Nit, with one example each.</li><li>How do you stay consistent across hundreds of items?</li><li>What do you do when the guidelines don&#x27;t cover a case?</li><li>What decides &quot;much better&quot; vs &quot;slightly better&quot; in a pairwise judgment?</li><li>What is length bias, and how do you guard against it?</li></ol><p><strong>AI literacy</strong></p><ol><li>Why can&#x27;t an AI feature spec promise &quot;always accurate&quot;?</li><li>What does p95 latency mean?</li><li>What is RAG, and what does &quot;grounded&quot; mean?</li><li>What is prompt injection?</li><li>What should an AI feature do when it fails?</li><li>Explain RLHF in 30 seconds.</li></ol><p><strong>About you</strong></p><ol><li>Your 60-second introduction.</li><li>&quot;You&#x27;ve never held a PM title. Why should we trust your product judgment?&quot;</li></ol>","secs":[],"words":253},{"id":"bk-your-30-day-plan-and-coaching-sessions","title":"Your 30-day plan and coaching sessions","html":"<p>Week 1 is the Bootcamp. Weeks 2–4 are this book. About an hour a day; the daily dose in the Flash Protocol applies throughout.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Week</th><th>Focus</th><th>Daily work</th><th>End-of-week check (with me)</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>7-Day Bootcamp</td><td>One bootcamp day per day + its exercises</td><td>&quot;Mock interview&quot;: 5 questions, graded</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Modules A, B + mnemonics</td><td>One module section + 1 scenario + 5 drills</td><td>&quot;Scenario drill&quot;: 3 fresh cases, graded Junior/Mid/Senior</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Modules C, D, E</td><td>One module section + 1 scenario + 5 drills</td><td>&quot;Grade my rationale&quot;: 3 full evaluations you write</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Integration and speed</td><td>2 timed evaluations a day (15 min each) + flashcards</td><td>Full mock interview + micro1&#x27;s free practice interview for a PM role</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Ready-to-apply signals</strong> (all five, consistently):</p><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.33567-0\"><span>Your rationales follow VREIF without thinking about it.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.33567-1\"><span>You can recite and unpack every mnemonic.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.33567-2\"><span>You score Senior on 3 consecutive scenario drills.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.33567-3\"><span>You finish a full evaluation in under 15 minutes.</span></label></li><li><label class=\"chk\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-k=\"bk-.33567-4\"><span>Your 60-second intro is natural and honest about your background.</span></label></li></ul><h4 id=\"how-coaching-sessions-with-me-work\">How coaching sessions with me work</h4><p>Start any chat with one of these commands. I&#x27;ll keep it fast, score you honestly, and push you to the next level.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Command</th><th>What happens</th><th>Length</th></tr><tr><td><strong>&quot;Flash me [module]&quot;</strong></td><td>2-minute summary, then 5 rapid-fire questions; I grade each and correct gaps</td><td>10 min</td></tr><tr><td><strong>&quot;Scenario drill&quot;</strong></td><td>A new case you haven&#x27;t seen; you write the rationale; I score it and show the Senior version</td><td>15 min</td></tr><tr><td><strong>&quot;Grade my rationale&quot;</strong></td><td>You paste a rationale; I review it like QA: severity, evidence, structure</td><td>10 min</td></tr><tr><td><strong>&quot;Mock interview&quot;</strong></td><td>Questions one at a time, feedback after each, overall level at the end</td><td>20–30 min</td></tr><tr><td><strong>&quot;Flashcards&quot;</strong></td><td>An interactive card deck in chat</td><td>5–10 min</td></tr><tr><td><strong>&quot;What am I weakest at?&quot;</strong></td><td>I review your recent answers and name your top 2 gaps with drills</td><td>5 min</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>One rule for practice:</strong> never paste real micro1 task content, guidelines or client data into practice sessions. Use your own past work, public examples, or cases I invent.</p>","secs":[{"id":"how-coaching-sessions-with-me-work","t":"How coaching sessions with me work"}],"words":284},{"id":"bk-flashcard-deck","title":"Flashcard deck","html":"<p>Cover the answer column. Cards you miss go back to daily review (Leitner box); cards you get right three times move to weekly. Say &quot;Flashcards&quot; in chat for an interactive version.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Front</th><th>Back</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>VREIF</td><td>Verdict, Rule, Evidence, Impact, Fix</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Evaluation order hook</td><td>&quot;I Can Always Use Clear, Tight Copy&quot;: Instructions, Constraints, Accuracy, Usefulness/audience, Clarity, Tone, Concision</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3F · 3C · 3T</td><td>Fluent-but-wrong, Filler, Fabrication · Constraint breach, Contradiction, sCope creep · Tone, Target audience, Truckling (sycophancy)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Severity hook</td><td>Trust (Critical), Task (Major), Time (Minor), Taste (Nit)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Four product risks</td><td>Value, Usability, Feasibility, Viability</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>PM vs project manager</td><td>PM: what and why (outcomes). Project manager: how and when (delivery)</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Output vs outcome</td><td>Output = what shipped; outcome = the change it caused</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>KPI formula</td><td>Numerator ÷ denominator, over a time window</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Counter-metric</td><td>A guardrail metric that catches gaming or harm when you push a main metric</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Vanity metric</td><td>Looks impressive but doesn&#x27;t drive decisions (e.g., total chats)</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>RICE</td><td>(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>MoSCoW</td><td>Must, Should, Could, Won&#x27;t (this time)</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>PRD hook</td><td>&quot;Pretty Good Notes Usually Require Fixing All Risky Releases&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>PRISM-SAD</td><td>Performance, Reliability, Integrity, Security, Maintainability, Scalability, Accessibility, Data privacy</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>INVEST</td><td>Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Given / When / Then</td><td>Scene, Act, Proof</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Good acceptance criteria</td><td>Binary, observable, complete, independent, solution-free</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Ambiguity trigger words</td><td>quickly, simple, intuitive, robust, seamless, etc., and/or, should</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Exec / Engineer / User needs</td><td>WHY + ASK / HOW + EDGE / WHAT + NEXT</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>BLUF</td><td>Bottom line up front</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>SCQA</td><td>Situation, Complication, Question, Answer</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Stakeholder update hook</td><td>&quot;Smart Captains Measure Risks And Navigate&quot;: Status, Changes, Metrics, Risks, Ask, Next</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Release notes hook</td><td>&quot;Happy News Is Fixed Always&quot;: Highlight, New, Improved, Fixed, Action required</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Buried lede</td><td>The main point appears too late for the reader to find</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Empty fluency</td><td>Polished sentences with no specific content</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>Gap, Rule, Ask, Act</td><td>The structure of an escalation note</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>Calibration</td><td>Aligning reviewers on what each score means, using shared examples</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>IAA</td><td>Inter-annotator agreement: how consistently reviewers give the same judgment</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>Length bias</td><td>Unfairly preferring longer responses</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>Pairwise strength</td><td>&quot;Much better&quot; = Major/Critical difference; &quot;slightly&quot; = Minor</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>p95 latency</td><td>95% of requests are at least this fast</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>RAG</td><td>Retrieve relevant documents, then generate an answer from them</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>Grounded</td><td>Every claim is supported by the source</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>Prompt injection</td><td>Input that tricks the AI into ignoring its instructions</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>Graceful degradation</td><td>A safe fallback when the AI fails or is slow</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>Drift</td><td>Real-world inputs or correct answers change over time, so quality drops</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>RLHF in one line</td><td>Humans compare outputs → reward model learns preferences → model is optimized toward them</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>Opportunity Solution Tree</td><td>Outcome → opportunities → solutions → experiments (Teresa Torres)</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>Good strategy (Rumelt)</td><td>Diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent actions</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>STAR ratio</td><td>Situation 20%, Task 10%, Action 60%, Result 10%</td></tr></table></div>","secs":[],"words":380},{"id":"bk-phase-2-roadmap-deeper-ai-product-engineering-la","title":"Phase 2 roadmap: deeper AI product engineering (later)","html":"<p>The AI Product Management curriculum you shared goes far deeper into engineering than the PM/PO evaluation role needs. Park these topics until you&#x27;ve landed the evaluation work. They&#x27;re the right next step toward AI Product Manager or AI systems roles, and they match the gaps your own dossier names.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Order</th><th>Topic</th><th>Why it matters for you</th><th>Start with</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Automated evaluation (LLM-as-a-judge, eval sets, RAG metrics such as faithfulness and context relevance)</td><td>Turns your manual evaluation skill into systems you can build</td><td>Build a 50-question eval set for one of your chatbots and score it weekly</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>RAG system design (chunking, hybrid search, re-ranking)</td><td>You already build knowledge-based bots; this is the engineering behind them</td><td>Rebuild one client bot&#x27;s knowledge base and measure grounding before and after</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Observability and cost tracking (logging prompts and outputs, token costs, latency)</td><td>Lets you prove quality and unit economics to clients</td><td>Add cost-per-conversation and p95 latency to one client dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Deployment safety (shadow testing, canary rollouts, rollback)</td><td>Safer changes to live client bots</td><td>Run a new prompt version on 10% of traffic before full rollout</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Governance (OWASP Top 10 for LLMs, privacy, EU AI Act basics)</td><td>Needed for enterprise and regulated clients</td><td>Write a one-page risk assessment for a clinic chatbot</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Fine-tuning basics (SFT, LoRA)</td><td>Understand when adapting a model beats prompting</td><td>Read and summarize one LoRA tutorial; don&#x27;t train yet</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Infrastructure (APIs at scale, containers, GPU serving)</td><td>Only if you move toward AI engineering roles</td><td>Optional</td></tr></table></div><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> learn each topic by applying it to a real system you already run. Your strength is practical systems work; keep the theory anchored to it.</p>","secs":[],"words":245}]}],"checks":[{"k":"bc-.9346-0","t":"Write 3 JTBD statements for users of a chatbot funnel you've built.","ch":"bc-day-1-product-management-foundations"},{"k":"bc-.9346-1","t":"Take one client project: write its problem statement, the output you shipped, and the outcome it caused, with a number.","ch":"bc-day-1-product-management-foundations"},{"k":"bc-.9346-2","t":"Define 2 KPIs from your CRM work as numerator ÷ denominator + time window.","ch":"bc-day-1-product-management-foundations"},{"k":"bc-.9346-3","t":"Use MoSCoW to scope an MVP for a \"missed-call text-back\" feature.","ch":"bc-day-1-product-management-foundations"},{"k":"bc-.9346-4","t":"Out loud, in under 60 seconds: \"What's the difference between a product manager and a project manager, and which are you?\"","ch":"bc-day-1-product-management-foundations"},{"k":"bc-.13589-0","t":"Write a one-page PRD for a CRM feature you built (lead routing, duplicate prevention or missed-call text-back), using all 9 sections.","ch":"bc-day-2-specifications"},{"k":"bc-.13589-1","t":"Write 3 user stories for it and check each against INVEST.","ch":"bc-day-2-specifications"},{"k":"bc-.13589-2","t":"Write acceptance criteria for one story in both formats, including 2 unhappy paths.","ch":"bc-day-2-specifications"},{"k":"bc-.13589-3","t":"List 5 edge cases and the expected behavior for each.","ch":"bc-day-2-specifications"},{"k":"bc-.13589-4","t":"Find one vague word in your PRD (\"quickly\", \"easily\") and replace it with a number.","ch":"bc-day-2-specifications"},{"k":"bc-.17568-0","t":"Write one change from your work three ways: executive, technical, end user.","ch":"bc-day-3-writing-for-every-audience"},{"k":"bc-.17568-1","t":"Write release notes for 3 changes to a funnel or bot, benefit first.","ch":"bc-day-3-writing-for-every-audience"},{"k":"bc-.17568-2","t":"Rewrite 3 error messages from a tool you use so they explain how to fix the problem.","ch":"bc-day-3-writing-for-every-audience"},{"k":"bc-.17568-3","t":"Write a BLUF stakeholder update for a client project, ending with a clear ask.","ch":"bc-day-3-writing-for-every-audience"},{"k":"bc-.17568-4","t":"Write a decision doc for why you chose one automation tool over another, with 2 rejected options.","ch":"bc-day-3-writing-for-every-audience"},{"k":"bc-.22966-0","t":"Write a 1–5 rubric with anchors for \"audience fit\".","ch":"bc-day-4-evaluating-content"},{"k":"bc-.22966-1","t":"Ask an AI assistant to write release notes with 3 constraints (e.g., under 120 words, exactly 3 bullets, include an action-required line). Evaluate it with the routine above.","ch":"bc-day-4-evaluating-content"},{"k":"bc-.22966-2","t":"Ask for two versions of an exec update; compare them side by side and pick one with a reason.","ch":"bc-day-4-evaluating-content"},{"k":"bc-.22966-3","t":"Find and name 3 failure modes in AI-written content you've seen at work.","ch":"bc-day-4-evaluating-content"},{"k":"bc-.22966-4","t":"Out loud: \"How do you keep your scoring consistent across 200 responses?\"","ch":"bc-day-4-evaluating-content"},{"k":"bc-.27874-0","t":"Rewrite the 4 weak rationales in the table above in your own words, using the formula.","ch":"bc-day-5-writing-rationales-that-hold-up"},{"k":"bc-.27874-1","t":"Ask an AI assistant for two stakeholder updates on the same project; write a full pairwise rationale.","ch":"bc-day-5-writing-rationales-that-hold-up"},{"k":"bc-.27874-2","t":"Write one escalation note for an ambiguous prompt you invent.","ch":"bc-day-5-writing-rationales-that-hold-up"},{"k":"bc-.27874-3","t":"Take a past client report you wrote; write a rationale scoring your own work on clarity and audience fit.","ch":"bc-day-5-writing-rationales-that-hold-up"},{"k":"bc-.27874-4","t":"Time yourself: one full rationale in under 10 minutes.","ch":"bc-day-5-writing-rationales-that-hold-up"},{"k":"bc-.32310-0","t":"Explain RLHF out loud in 60 seconds to a non-technical friend.","ch":"bc-day-6-how-ai-models-learn-from-your-judgments"},{"k":"bc-.32310-1","t":"Write one demonstration: a prompt plus the ideal response, for a stakeholder update.","ch":"bc-day-6-how-ai-models-learn-from-your-judgments"},{"k":"bc-.32310-2","t":"Ask an AI assistant a CRM question with a false premise (\"Why does HubSpot delete contacts after 30 days?\"). Note whether it corrects you or goes along with it.","ch":"bc-day-6-how-ai-models-learn-from-your-judgments"},{"k":"bc-.32310-3","t":"Start your own edge-case log: 5 tricky situations from your CRM tasks and how you'd decide them.","ch":"bc-day-6-how-ai-models-learn-from-your-judgments"},{"k":"bc-.32310-4","t":"Out loud: \"Why do evaluators need to avoid length bias?\"","ch":"bc-day-6-how-ai-models-learn-from-your-judgments"},{"k":"bc-.38885-0","t":"Read the job description again; list its 10 required skills and one story for each.","ch":"bc-day-7-interview-readiness"},{"k":"bc-.38885-1","t":"Rehearse the 60-second intro 3 times.","ch":"bc-day-7-interview-readiness"},{"k":"bc-.38885-2","t":"Answer all 10 practice questions out loud.","ch":"bc-day-7-interview-readiness"},{"k":"bc-.38885-3","t":"Re-read the glossary; cover the right column and define each term.","ch":"bc-day-7-interview-readiness"},{"k":"bc-.38885-4","t":"Do one full evaluation exercise (Day 4 routine + Day 5 rationale) in under 15 minutes.","ch":"bc-day-7-interview-readiness"},{"k":"bc-.38885-5","t":"Set up: quiet room, good light, stable internet, one screen, phone away.","ch":"bc-day-7-interview-readiness"},{"k":"bk-.11971-0","t":"Take one of your client projects. Write the four risks and how each was (or wasn't) handled.","ch":"bk-module-a-senior-product-thinking"},{"k":"bk-.11971-1","t":"Build a metric tree for a clinic's booking funnel, with one counter-metric.","ch":"bk-module-a-senior-product-thinking"},{"k":"bk-.11971-2","t":"Write a trade-off statement for choosing speed over accuracy in a chatbot's first version.","ch":"bk-module-a-senior-product-thinking"},{"k":"bk-.11971-3","t":"Spot the vanity metric in this claim: \"Our bot handled 40,000 messages last month.\" What would you ask for instead?","ch":"bk-module-a-senior-product-thinking"},{"k":"bk-.15410-0","t":"Ask an AI assistant to write a PRD for \"missed-call text-back\". Run the 10-point stress test; write each finding in VREIF format.","ch":"bk-module-b-senior-spec-craft"},{"k":"bk-.15410-1","t":"Rewrite 5 ambiguous requirements from any real tool's help docs into precise ones.","ch":"bk-module-b-senior-spec-craft"},{"k":"bk-.15410-2","t":"For a lead pipeline, list every state a lead can be in and what moves it to the next one.","ch":"bk-module-b-senior-spec-craft"},{"k":"bk-.15410-3","t":"Write the \"what's missing\" list for a spec that only has a feature description.","ch":"bk-module-b-senior-spec-craft"},{"k":"bk-.18146-0","t":"Rewrite one paragraph from an old client report three times: exec, technical, end user.","ch":"bk-module-c-senior-writing-judgment"},{"k":"bk-.18146-1","t":"Take 3 real release notes from apps you use. Score each on audience fit and call to action, using VREIF.","ch":"bk-module-c-senior-writing-judgment"},{"k":"bk-.18146-2","t":"Write a 5-line outage notice for a chatbot that sent wrong prices for 2 hours.","ch":"bk-module-c-senior-writing-judgment"},{"k":"bk-.18146-3","t":"Find a buried lede in any email you've received this week and rewrite it BLUF.","ch":"bk-module-c-senior-writing-judgment"},{"k":"bk-.21207-0","t":"Classify 10 findings from your earlier exercises by severity.","ch":"bk-module-d-senior-evaluation"},{"k":"bk-.21207-1","t":"Write a 4-dimension rubric with anchors for \"stakeholder update quality\".","ch":"bk-module-d-senior-evaluation"},{"k":"bk-.21207-2","t":"Write a \"Gap, Rule, Ask, Act\" note for: \"Prompt says 'brief'; response is 180 words; guidelines don't define brief.\"","ch":"bk-module-d-senior-evaluation"},{"k":"bk-.21207-3","t":"Do one pairwise comparison and state the deciding factor and what would flip it.","ch":"bk-module-d-senior-evaluation"},{"k":"bk-.25168-0","t":"Acceptable error rate and how it's measured","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25168-1","t":"Evaluation set and quality metrics","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25168-2","t":"Latency and cost limits","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25168-3","t":"Grounding sources (if it answers from documents)","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25168-4","t":"Guardrails: what it must never do","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25168-5","t":"Fallback and human escalation","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25168-6","t":"Monitoring for drift after launch","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25168-7","t":"How users are told it's AI and what to do when it's wrong","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25501-0","t":"Ask an AI assistant for a one-page spec of \"AI reply suggestions for a clinic's Messenger inbox\". Run the AI feature checklist and list every gap.","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25501-1","t":"Write an exec update for an AI chatbot launch that communicates uncertainty honestly.","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.25501-2","t":"Explain p95 latency and RAG to a non-technical client in 3 sentences each.","ch":"bk-module-e-ai-product-literacy-for-pm-po-evaluator"},{"k":"bk-.33567-0","t":"Your rationales follow VREIF without thinking about it.","ch":"bk-your-30-day-plan-and-coaching-sessions"},{"k":"bk-.33567-1","t":"You can recite and unpack every mnemonic.","ch":"bk-your-30-day-plan-and-coaching-sessions"},{"k":"bk-.33567-2","t":"You score Senior on 3 consecutive scenario drills.","ch":"bk-your-30-day-plan-and-coaching-sessions"},{"k":"bk-.33567-3","t":"You finish a full evaluation in under 15 minutes.","ch":"bk-your-30-day-plan-and-coaching-sessions"},{"k":"bk-.33567-4","t":"Your 60-second intro is natural and honest about your background.","ch":"bk-your-30-day-plan-and-coaching-sessions"}],"cards":[{"id":"fc1","f":"VREIF","b":"Verdict, Rule, Evidence, Impact, Fix","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc2","f":"Evaluation order hook","b":"\"I Can Always Use Clear, Tight Copy\": Instructions, Constraints, Accuracy, Usefulness/audience, Clarity, Tone, Concision","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc3","f":"3F · 3C · 3T","b":"Fluent-but-wrong, Filler, Fabrication · Constraint breach, Contradiction, sCope creep · Tone, Target audience, Truckling (sycophancy)","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc4","f":"Severity hook","b":"Trust (Critical), Task (Major), Time (Minor), Taste (Nit)","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc5","f":"Four product risks","b":"Value, Usability, Feasibility, Viability","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc6","f":"PM vs project manager","b":"PM: what and why (outcomes). Project manager: how and when (delivery)","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc7","f":"Output vs outcome","b":"Output = what shipped; outcome = the change it caused","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc8","f":"KPI formula","b":"Numerator ÷ denominator, over a time window","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc9","f":"Counter-metric","b":"A guardrail metric that catches gaming or harm when you push a main metric","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc10","f":"Vanity metric","b":"Looks impressive but doesn't drive decisions (e.g., total chats)","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc11","f":"RICE","b":"(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc12","f":"MoSCoW","b":"Must, Should, Could, Won't (this time)","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc13","f":"PRD hook","b":"\"Pretty Good Notes Usually Require Fixing All Risky Releases\"","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc14","f":"PRISM-SAD","b":"Performance, Reliability, Integrity, Security, Maintainability, Scalability, Accessibility, Data privacy","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc15","f":"INVEST","b":"Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc16","f":"Given / When / Then","b":"Scene, Act, Proof","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc17","f":"Good acceptance criteria","b":"Binary, observable, complete, independent, solution-free","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc18","f":"Ambiguity trigger words","b":"quickly, simple, intuitive, robust, seamless, etc., and/or, should","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc19","f":"Exec / Engineer / User needs","b":"WHY + ASK / HOW + EDGE / WHAT + NEXT","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc20","f":"BLUF","b":"Bottom line up front","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc21","f":"SCQA","b":"Situation, Complication, Question, Answer","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc22","f":"Stakeholder update hook","b":"\"Smart Captains Measure Risks And Navigate\": Status, Changes, Metrics, Risks, Ask, Next","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc23","f":"Release notes hook","b":"\"Happy News Is Fixed Always\": Highlight, New, Improved, Fixed, Action required","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc24","f":"Buried lede","b":"The main point appears too late for the reader to find","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc25","f":"Empty fluency","b":"Polished sentences with no specific content","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc26","f":"Gap, Rule, Ask, Act","b":"The structure of an escalation note","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc27","f":"Calibration","b":"Aligning reviewers on what each score means, using shared examples","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc28","f":"IAA","b":"Inter-annotator agreement: how consistently reviewers give the same judgment","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc29","f":"Length bias","b":"Unfairly preferring longer responses","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc30","f":"Pairwise strength","b":"\"Much better\" = Major/Critical difference; \"slightly\" = Minor","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc31","f":"p95 latency","b":"95% of requests are at least this fast","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc32","f":"RAG","b":"Retrieve relevant documents, then generate an answer from them","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc33","f":"Grounded","b":"Every claim is supported by the source","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc34","f":"Prompt injection","b":"Input that tricks the AI into ignoring its instructions","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc35","f":"Graceful degradation","b":"A safe fallback when the AI fails or is slow","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc36","f":"Drift","b":"Real-world inputs or correct answers change over time, so quality drops","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc37","f":"RLHF in one line","b":"Humans compare outputs → reward model learns preferences → model is optimized toward them","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc38","f":"Opportunity Solution Tree","b":"Outcome → opportunities → solutions → experiments (Teresa Torres)","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc39","f":"Good strategy (Rumelt)","b":"Diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent actions","deck":"core"},{"id":"fc40","f":"STAR ratio","b":"Situation 20%, Task 10%, Action 60%, Result 10%","deck":"core"},{"id":"gl-backlog","f":"Backlog","b":"The ordered list of work items (stories) for a product","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-discovery","f":"Discovery","b":"Learning what to build before building it","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-epic","f":"Epic","b":"A large piece of work split into smaller stories","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-jobs-to-be-done-jtbd","f":"Jobs to Be Done (JTBD)","b":"The progress a user is trying to make; \"When… I want to… so I can…\"","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-kpi","f":"KPI","b":"Key performance indicator: a defined metric (numerator ÷ denominator, over time)","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-leading-lagging-indicator","f":"Leading / lagging indicator","b":"Leading predicts results; lagging confirms them afterward","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-moscow","f":"MoSCoW","b":"Must / Should / Could / Won't prioritization","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-mvp","f":"MVP","b":"Minimum viable product: the smallest thing that tests whether a solution creates value","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-north-star-metric","f":"North Star Metric","b":"The single metric that best captures value delivered to users","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-okr","f":"OKR","b":"Objective + measurable Key Results","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-outcome-vs-output","f":"Outcome vs output","b":"Outcome = change caused; output = thing shipped","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-persona","f":"Persona","b":"A research-based profile of a user type","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-product-owner","f":"Product Owner","b":"Owns the backlog and its value, usually in Scrum","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-rice","f":"RICE","b":"(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-stakeholder","f":"Stakeholder","b":"Anyone affected by or influencing the product","deck":"glossary","g":"Product management"},{"id":"gl-acceptance-criteria","f":"Acceptance criteria","b":"Testable conditions a story must meet to be done","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-assumption","f":"Assumption","b":"Something treated as true without proof yet","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-constraint","f":"Constraint","b":"A limit the solution must respect (budget, platform, law)","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-definition-of-done-dod","f":"Definition of Done (DoD)","b":"The shared checklist for when work is finished","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-definition-of-ready-dor","f":"Definition of Ready (DoR)","b":"The checklist for when work can start","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-dependency","f":"Dependency","b":"Something the work relies on (another team, an API)","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-edge-case","f":"Edge case","b":"A valid but unusual situation","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-functional-requirement","f":"Functional requirement","b":"What the system must do","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-gherkin-given-when-then","f":"Gherkin / Given-When-Then","b":"Scenario format for acceptance criteria","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-happy-unhappy-path","f":"Happy / unhappy path","b":"Everything goes right / something goes wrong","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-invest","f":"INVEST","b":"Good story test: Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-non-functional-requirement","f":"Non-functional requirement","b":"How well it must work: speed, security, reliability, accessibility","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-non-goals-out-of-scope","f":"Non-goals / out of scope","b":"What the work deliberately won't do","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-prd","f":"PRD","b":"Product Requirements Document: what to build and why","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-uat","f":"UAT","b":"User acceptance testing: users confirm it meets requirements","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-user-story","f":"User story","b":"\"As a… I want… so that…\"","deck":"glossary","g":"Specifications"},{"id":"gl-adr","f":"ADR","b":"Architecture Decision Record: a short record of a technical decision","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-bluf","f":"BLUF","b":"Bottom line up front","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-changelog","f":"Changelog","b":"Complete technical list of changes","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-microcopy","f":"Microcopy","b":"Small UI text: buttons, errors, tooltips","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-plain-language","f":"Plain language","b":"Short sentences, common words, active voice","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-pyramid-principle","f":"Pyramid Principle","b":"Main point first, then grouped support","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-release-notes","f":"Release notes","b":"User-facing summary of what changed and why it matters","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-rfc","f":"RFC","b":"Request for Comments: a proposal shared for feedback","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-scqa","f":"SCQA","b":"Situation, Complication, Question, Answer","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-tone-voice","f":"Tone / voice","b":"Voice = consistent personality; tone = how it adapts to the moment","deck":"glossary","g":"Product writing"},{"id":"gl-adjudication","f":"Adjudication","b":"A senior reviewer resolving disagreements","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-anchor","f":"Anchor","b":"The written description of each score level","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-annotation-labeling","f":"Annotation / labeling","b":"Adding structured judgments to data","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-calibration","f":"Calibration","b":"Aligning reviewers on what each score means","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-dpo","f":"DPO","b":"Direct Preference Optimization: training on preference pairs without a reward model","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-gold-set","f":"Gold set","b":"Pre-scored items used to check accuracy","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-hallucination","f":"Hallucination","b":"Confident, fluent, false content","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-holistic-vs-analytic-scoring","f":"Holistic vs analytic scoring","b":"One overall score vs one per dimension","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-instruction-following","f":"Instruction following","b":"Doing exactly what the prompt asked","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-inter-annotator-agreement-iaa","f":"Inter-annotator agreement (IAA)","b":"How often different reviewers give the same judgment","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-length-bias","f":"Length bias","b":"Unfairly preferring longer responses","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-likert-scale","f":"Likert scale","b":"A rating scale like 1–5","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-pairwise-comparison","f":"Pairwise comparison","b":"Choosing the better of two responses","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-preference-data","f":"Preference data","b":"Records of which response humans preferred","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-rationale","f":"Rationale","b":"Written justification for a score","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-red-teaming","f":"Red teaming","b":"Deliberately trying to make a model fail","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-reward-hacking","f":"Reward hacking","b":"A model pleasing the scorer without truly improving","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-reward-model","f":"Reward model","b":"A model trained to predict human preferences","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-rlhf","f":"RLHF","b":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-rubric","f":"Rubric","b":"Scoring guide with criteria and level descriptions","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-sft","f":"SFT","b":"Supervised fine-tuning on expert-written examples","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-sycophancy","f":"Sycophancy","b":"Agreeing with a user's wrong premise","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"gl-token","f":"Token","b":"The unit of text a model reads and writes (a word or part of one)","deck":"glossary","g":"Evaluation and AI"},{"id":"mn-rationale-shape","f":"\"Very Rarely Experts Ignore Facts\" (VREIF)","b":"Verdict → Rule → Evidence → Impact → Fix","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Rationale shape"},{"id":"mn-evaluation-order","f":"\"I Can Always Use Clear, Tight Copy\"","b":"Instructions → Constraints → Accuracy → Usefulness & audience → Clarity → Tone → Concision","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Evaluation order"},{"id":"mn-failure-modes","f":"\"3F · 3C · 3T\"","b":"Fluent-but-wrong, Filler, Fabrication (hallucination) · Constraint breach, Contradiction, sCope creep · wrong Tone, wrong Target audience, Truckling (sycophancy)","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Failure modes"},{"id":"mn-severity","f":"\"Trust, Task, Time, Taste\"","b":"Critical breaks trust (false, unsafe) · Major breaks the task (missed requirement) · Minor costs the reader time (unclear) · Nit is taste (style)","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Severity"},{"id":"mn-audiences","f":"\"Execs: WHY + ASK. Engineers: HOW + EDGE. Users: WHAT + NEXT.\"","b":"What each audience needs first","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Audiences"},{"id":"mn-prd-sections","f":"\"Pretty Good Notes Usually Require Fixing All Risky Releases\"","b":"Problem, Goals, Non-goals, Users, Requirements, Flows, Assumptions/constraints, Risks, Release","deck":"mnemonic","g":"PRD sections"},{"id":"mn-non-functional-requirements","f":"\"PRISM-SAD\"","b":"Performance, Reliability, Integrity (data), Security, Maintainability · Scalability, Accessibility, Data privacy","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Non-functional requirements"},{"id":"mn-good-user-story","f":"INVEST","b":"Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Good user story"},{"id":"mn-acceptance-criteria","f":"\"Scene, Act, Proof\"","b":"Given (scene) → When (act) → Then (proof)","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Acceptance criteria"},{"id":"mn-product-risks-marty-cagan","f":"\"Want it? Use it? Build it? Should we?\"","b":"Value, Usability, Feasibility, Business viability","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Product risks (Marty Cagan)"},{"id":"mn-prioritization","f":"RICE = \"Reach Into Customers' Experience\"","b":"(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Prioritization"},{"id":"mn-scope","f":"MoSCoW","b":"Must, Should, Could, Won't (this time)","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Scope"},{"id":"mn-stakeholder-update","f":"\"Smart Captains Measure Risks And Navigate\"","b":"Status, Changes, Metrics, Risks, Ask, Next","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Stakeholder update"},{"id":"mn-release-notes","f":"\"Happy News Is Fixed Always\"","b":"Highlight, New, Improved, Fixed, Action required","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Release notes"},{"id":"mn-decision-doc","f":"\"Don't Choose Options Randomly; Trade-offs Count\"","b":"Decision, Context, Options, Rationale, Trade-offs, Consequences","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Decision doc"},{"id":"mn-exec-writing","f":"BLUF","b":"Bottom line up front","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Exec writing"},{"id":"mn-proposal-logic","f":"SCQA = \"Some Clients Question Answers\"","b":"Situation, Complication, Question, Answer","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Proposal logic"},{"id":"mn-metrics","f":"\"Count it, cap it, clock it\"","b":"Numerator ÷ denominator, over a time window","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Metrics"},{"id":"mn-interview-stories","f":"STAR 2-1-6-1","b":"Situation 20%, Task 10%, Action 60%, Result 10%","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Interview stories"},{"id":"mn-ambiguity-note","f":"\"Gap, Rule, Ask, Act\"","b":"The ambiguity, the missing rule, the decision needed, what you did meanwhile","deck":"mnemonic","g":"Ambiguity note"}],"mnemonics":[{"id":"mn-rationale-shape","fw":"Rationale shape","hook":"\"Very Rarely Experts Ignore Facts\" (VREIF)","un":"Verdict → Rule → Evidence → Impact → Fix"},{"id":"mn-evaluation-order","fw":"Evaluation order","hook":"\"I Can Always Use Clear, Tight Copy\"","un":"Instructions → Constraints → Accuracy → Usefulness & audience → Clarity → Tone → Concision"},{"id":"mn-failure-modes","fw":"Failure modes","hook":"\"3F · 3C · 3T\"","un":"Fluent-but-wrong, Filler, Fabrication (hallucination) · Constraint breach, Contradiction, sCope creep · wrong Tone, wrong Target audience, Truckling (sycophancy)"},{"id":"mn-severity","fw":"Severity","hook":"\"Trust, Task, Time, Taste\"","un":"Critical breaks trust (false, unsafe) · Major breaks the task (missed requirement) · Minor costs the reader time (unclear) · Nit is taste (style)"},{"id":"mn-audiences","fw":"Audiences","hook":"\"Execs: WHY + ASK. Engineers: HOW + EDGE. Users: WHAT + NEXT.\"","un":"What each audience needs first"},{"id":"mn-prd-sections","fw":"PRD sections","hook":"\"Pretty Good Notes Usually Require Fixing All Risky Releases\"","un":"Problem, Goals, Non-goals, Users, Requirements, Flows, Assumptions/constraints, Risks, Release"},{"id":"mn-non-functional-requirements","fw":"Non-functional requirements","hook":"\"PRISM-SAD\"","un":"Performance, Reliability, Integrity (data), Security, Maintainability · Scalability, Accessibility, Data privacy"},{"id":"mn-good-user-story","fw":"Good user story","hook":"INVEST","un":"Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable"},{"id":"mn-acceptance-criteria","fw":"Acceptance criteria","hook":"\"Scene, Act, Proof\"","un":"Given (scene) → When (act) → Then (proof)"},{"id":"mn-product-risks-marty-cagan","fw":"Product risks (Marty Cagan)","hook":"\"Want it? Use it? Build it? Should we?\"","un":"Value, Usability, Feasibility, Business viability"},{"id":"mn-prioritization","fw":"Prioritization","hook":"RICE = \"Reach Into Customers' Experience\"","un":"(Reach × Impact × Confidence) ÷ Effort"},{"id":"mn-scope","fw":"Scope","hook":"MoSCoW","un":"Must, Should, Could, Won't (this time)"},{"id":"mn-stakeholder-update","fw":"Stakeholder update","hook":"\"Smart Captains Measure Risks And Navigate\"","un":"Status, Changes, Metrics, Risks, Ask, Next"},{"id":"mn-release-notes","fw":"Release notes","hook":"\"Happy News Is Fixed Always\"","un":"Highlight, New, Improved, Fixed, Action required"},{"id":"mn-decision-doc","fw":"Decision doc","hook":"\"Don't Choose Options Randomly; Trade-offs Count\"","un":"Decision, Context, Options, Rationale, Trade-offs, Consequences"},{"id":"mn-exec-writing","fw":"Exec writing","hook":"BLUF","un":"Bottom line up front"},{"id":"mn-proposal-logic","fw":"Proposal logic","hook":"SCQA = \"Some Clients Question Answers\"","un":"Situation, Complication, Question, Answer"},{"id":"mn-metrics","fw":"Metrics","hook":"\"Count it, cap it, clock it\"","un":"Numerator ÷ denominator, over a time window"},{"id":"mn-interview-stories","fw":"Interview stories","hook":"STAR 2-1-6-1","un":"Situation 20%, Task 10%, Action 60%, Result 10%"},{"id":"mn-ambiguity-note","fw":"Ambiguity note","hook":"\"Gap, Rule, Ask, Act\"","un":"The ambiguity, the missing rule, the decision needed, what you did meanwhile"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"sc1","n":1,"t":"The fluent release note","setup":"Prompt given to the AI: \"Release notes for clinic receptionists. Under 80 words. Must say what they need to do differently.\" AI response: \"We're thrilled to unveil our reimagined scheduling experience! Powered by intelligent automation, appointment management is now smoother, faster and more delightful than ever. Enjoy the future of front-desk efficiency!\"","answers":[{"lvl":"Junior","txt":"\"Too much marketing language. 2/5.\""},{"lvl":"Mid","txt":"\"Fails the requirement to say what users must do differently; tone is promotional. 2/5.\""},{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"\"2/5. Instruction following (Major): the prompt requires what receptionists must do differently; the response never says what changed or what to do. Empty fluency: 'smoother, faster and more delightful' describes no specific change. Audience: receptionists need the practical change, not 'the future of front-desk efficiency'. Constraint: 29 words, within limit. Fix: name the actual change and the one action needed.\""}],"score":2,"pairwise":false},{"id":"sc2","n":2,"t":"The over-long exec update","setup":"Prompt: \"Exec update on the CRM migration. 5 bullets max. Lead with status.\" Response: 7 bullets; status (\"at risk\") appears in bullet 6; detailed field-mapping notes in bullets 1–4.","answers":[{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"\"2/5. Constraint (Major): 7 bullets against a 5-bullet limit. Instruction (Major): status must lead; it's in bullet 6, so an executive skimming would miss that the migration is at risk. Audience: bullets 1–4 are field-mapping detail an executive can't act on. Strength: the risk itself is clearly explained in bullet 6. Fix: lead with 'At risk: launch may slip 1 week due to X; decision needed on Y by Friday', then 4 short bullets.\""}],"score":2,"pairwise":false},{"id":"sc3","n":3,"t":"Pairwise: two acceptance criteria sets","setup":"Story: \"As a sales rep, I want leads auto-assigned so I can respond fast.\" A: \"Leads are assigned quickly and fairly to reps.\" B: \"Given a new lead, when it's created during business hours, then it's assigned round-robin to an active rep within 30 seconds. Given no rep is active, then it's assigned to the sales manager and flagged.\"","answers":[{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"\"B is much better. Deciding factor: testability. A uses 'quickly' and 'fairly', neither of which can be tested. B defines timing (30 seconds), method (round-robin), condition (business hours) and an unhappy path (no active rep). B's gap: it doesn't cover leads created outside business hours. What would flip it: nothing. A can't be tested as written.\""}],"score":null,"pairwise":true},{"id":"sc4","n":4,"t":"The hallucinated feature","setup":"Prompt: \"Summarize this changelog for customers.\" The changelog lists: CSV export added, search bug fixed. Response: \"New: CSV and PDF export. Fixed: search bug. Improved: dashboard loads 2× faster.\"","answers":[{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"\"1/5. Accuracy (Critical): 'PDF export' and 'dashboard loads 2× faster' are not in the source changelog. Customers would look for features that don't exist, which damages trust. The correct items (CSV export, search fix) are present. Fix: remove anything not in the source.\""}],"score":1,"pairwise":false},{"id":"sc5","n":5,"t":"Complete but unusable","setup":"Prompt: \"Explain the new duplicate-merge feature to small-business owners.\" Response: a correct, complete explanation of normalized E.164 matching, merge precedence and audit logging.","answers":[{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"\"3/5. Accuracy and completeness: strong; the logic is correct. Audience fit (Major): small-business owners don't need E.164 or merge precedence; they need 'you'll stop seeing the same customer twice, and nothing is lost'. Usefulness: no next step. Fix: lead with the benefit, one sentence on how it works, and where to review merged contacts.\""}],"score":3,"pairwise":false},{"id":"sc6","n":6,"t":"Ambiguous guideline","setup":"Situation: The rubric says penalize \"unprofessional tone\". The response, for a youth sports club newsletter, is casual and uses one emoji.","answers":[{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"Scores tone against the intended audience (casual is appropriate for a club newsletter), notes the judgment explicitly, and writes a \"Gap, Rule, Ask, Act\" note: \"Gap: 'unprofessional' isn't defined relative to audience. Rule: none for informal audiences. Ask: should tone be judged against the audience in the prompt? Act: judged it appropriate for the stated audience; flagged.\""}],"score":null,"pairwise":false},{"id":"sc7","n":7,"t":"The AI feature spec","setup":"Spec excerpt: \"The AI assistant will always answer patient questions accurately using our clinic information.\"","answers":[{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"\"'Always accurate' is not achievable or testable (define an accuracy target and how it's measured). No grounding source or update process for 'clinic information'. No fallback or human handoff for medical questions (Critical, given patient safety). No guardrail stating what it must never do (diagnose, give dosage advice).\""}],"score":null,"pairwise":false},{"id":"sc8","n":8,"t":"Interview curveball","setup":"Question: \"A response is beautifully written and accurate, but 30% over the word limit. Another is plain, within the limit, and meets every requirement. Which is better?\"","answers":[{"lvl":"Senior","txt":"\"The second, unless the guidelines say otherwise. The word limit is an explicit constraint, so exceeding it by 30% is a Major issue, while plain style is at most a Minor one. I'd note the first response's strengths in the rationale, because they show the model can write well, but the deciding factor is instruction compliance. If the limit were soft, I'd check the guidelines before scoring.\""}],"score":null,"pairwise":false}],"drills":[{"id":"dr1","q":"Product manager vs project manager, in two sentences.","cat":"Foundations"},{"id":"dr2","q":"Output vs outcome, with an example from your work.","cat":"Foundations"},{"id":"dr3","q":"What are the four product risks?","cat":"Foundations"},{"id":"dr4","q":"What's a counter-metric, and why does it matter?","cat":"Foundations"},{"id":"dr5","q":"When would you use MoSCoW instead of RICE?","cat":"Foundations"},{"id":"dr6","q":"What makes an acceptance criterion testable?","cat":"Specs"},{"id":"dr7","q":"Name five words that signal ambiguity in a spec.","cat":"Specs"},{"id":"dr8","q":"Functional vs non-functional requirement, with one example each.","cat":"Specs"},{"id":"dr9","q":"What belongs in a \"non-goals\" section, and why include it?","cat":"Specs"},{"id":"dr10","q":"What's missing from a spec that only describes the happy path?","cat":"Specs"},{"id":"dr11","q":"What does an executive need in the first line?","cat":"Writing"},{"id":"dr12","q":"What's a buried lede? How do you name it in a rationale?","cat":"Writing"},{"id":"dr13","q":"How should an error message be written?","cat":"Writing"},{"id":"dr14","q":"Changelog vs release notes?","cat":"Writing"},{"id":"dr15","q":"What's \"empty fluency\"?","cat":"Writing"},{"id":"dr16","q":"Recite VREIF and explain each step.","cat":"Evaluation"},{"id":"dr17","q":"Recite the evaluation order (\"I Can Always Use Clear, Tight Copy\").","cat":"Evaluation"},{"id":"dr18","q":"Critical vs Major vs Minor vs Nit, with one example each.","cat":"Evaluation"},{"id":"dr19","q":"How do you stay consistent across hundreds of items?","cat":"Evaluation"},{"id":"dr20","q":"What do you do when the guidelines don't cover a case?","cat":"Evaluation"},{"id":"dr21","q":"What decides \"much better\" vs \"slightly better\" in a pairwise judgment?","cat":"Evaluation"},{"id":"dr22","q":"What is length bias, and how do you guard against it?","cat":"Evaluation"},{"id":"dr23","q":"Why can't an AI feature spec promise \"always accurate\"?","cat":"AI literacy"},{"id":"dr24","q":"What does p95 latency mean?","cat":"AI literacy"},{"id":"dr25","q":"What is RAG, and what does \"grounded\" mean?","cat":"AI literacy"},{"id":"dr26","q":"What is prompt injection?","cat":"AI literacy"},{"id":"dr27","q":"What should an AI feature do when it fails?","cat":"AI literacy"},{"id":"dr28","q":"Explain RLHF in 30 seconds.","cat":"AI literacy"},{"id":"dr29","q":"Your 60-second introduction.","cat":"About you"},{"id":"dr30","q":"\"You've never held a PM title. Why should we trust your product judgment?\"","cat":"About you"},{"id":"dr31","q":"Walk me through a specification you wrote. What made it good?","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr32","q":"What's the difference between a PRD and a user story? When would you use each?","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr33","q":"Write acceptance criteria for a password reset feature. (Practice on paper: include unhappy paths.)","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr34","q":"How would you judge whether release notes are right for end users vs. engineers?","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr35","q":"Here are two stakeholder updates. Which is better, and why? (Use the rationale formula.)","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr36","q":"How do you tell when a well-written response doesn't actually answer the prompt?","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr37","q":"A response is accurate but 40% over the word limit. How do you score it?","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr38","q":"How do you stay consistent when scoring hundreds of responses?","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr39","q":"What do you do when the guidelines don't cover a case?","cat":"Interview"},{"id":"dr40","q":"What makes an executive update effective?","cat":"Interview"}],"plan":[{"wk":1,"focus":"7-Day Bootcamp","daily":"One bootcamp day per day + its exercises","check":"\"Mock interview\": 5 questions, graded"},{"wk":2,"focus":"Modules A, B + mnemonics","daily":"One module section + 1 scenario + 5 drills","check":"\"Scenario drill\": 3 fresh cases, graded Junior/Mid/Senior"},{"wk":3,"focus":"Modules C, D, E","daily":"One module section + 1 scenario + 5 drills","check":"\"Grade my rationale\": 3 full evaluations you write"},{"wk":4,"focus":"Integration and speed","daily":"2 timed evaluations a day (15 min each) + flashcards","check":"Full mock interview + micro1's free practice interview for a PM role"}],"palace":[{"room":"TESDA / UPH classroom","what":"Foundations: problem before solution, outcomes vs outputs","pic":"A whiteboard: \"PROBLEM\" written big, \"solution\" small underneath"},{"room":"Netmarks office, GSK DMS project","what":"Specs: PRD sections, acceptance criteria, Definition of Done","pic":"You at a UAT table, ticking a printed checklist: Given / When / Then"},{"room":"TeleTech data center","what":"Non-functional requirements: performance, reliability, security","pic":"Server racks with labels: SPEED, UPTIME, LOCK, SCALE"},{"room":"DBM training room (Incuventure)","what":"Audience writing: exec vs technical vs end user","pic":"Three chairs: a director, an engineer, a clerk, each holding a different version of your slides"},{"room":"Your Chatbot Authority desk","what":"Product thinking: JTBD, metrics, prioritization, trade-offs","pic":"A client lead report: 6 hrs → 7 min circled in red"},{"room":"Your micro1 workspace","what":"Evaluation: rubric, verdict → rule → evidence → impact → fix","pic":"A scorecard with five boxes filled left to right"}]};